+summary: 'Check a verifier-gated cascade against a small human-labeled sample the verifier never sees:
+  the verifier''s own numbers can hide most of the error. In one study the error measured through the
+  verifier stayed near 3% while true error swung 14-32%, and in a production text-to-SQL pipeline a judge
+  nobody had checked agreed with human labels at kappa 0.04 on the cases it flagged (0.42 on a random
+  sample). A frontier verifier cuts misses but escalates more than the true error rate, erasing the saving;
+  never train the cheap model on verifier rejects or accepted outputs. The verifier''s labels cannot replace
+  the gold sample (when it is no more accurate than the model it checks, they can at most halve the gold
+  labels needed), and the verifier itself drifts with silent version and prompt changes, so keep a frozen
+  human-labeled anchor set as well.'
+how: '1. Start from the baseline: record the cascade''s error as measured through the verifier (accept
+  and escalation rates, error on accepted items as the verifier sees it) and its cost per item, and run
+  the single strong model on the same traffic as the comparison arm. 2. Label a rolling sample (e.g. 100
+  cases a week) of delivered answers by hand or by the environment, drawn at random from live traffic
+  and stratified by the verifier''s verdict (accepted, escalated) or its confidence, with each stratum''s
+  share set in proportion to its size: in one study 200 labels gave an RMSE of 2.46 points with proportional
+  allocation against 4.56 with equal labels per stratum. Never build the gold set only from cases the
+  verifier flagged: a production judge''s agreement with humans was kappa 0.04 on such a set and 0.42
+  on a uniform-random one. Have two people label at least part of it and adjudicate, since gold is noisy
+  too (pre-consensus kappa 0.87 on 200 cases in that audit, and 21.7-25.5% of one public benchmark''s
+  expert-written gold SQL were flagged as candidate errors). 3. Score the cascade''s delivered answers
+  on that sample separately from the verifier''s own accept rate: true error per stratum and overall (weighted
+  by stratum size) with a 95% interval, plus the verifier''s miss rate (wrong answers it accepted) and
+  false-reject rate. In one study the verifier-measured error stayed near 3% while gold error on 300 items
+  swung 14-32%; in the production audit even the repaired judge''s raw faithful rate (84.3%) moved to
+  about 90.6% once reweighted with a 200-case random sample. 4. Use the verifier''s labels only through
+  a bias-corrected estimator (prediction-powered inference, or a correction by the miss and false-reject
+  rates measured on the gold sample), and do not count on them to shrink the gold sample: when the verifier
+  is no more accurate than the model it checks, no unbiased method can cut the gold labels needed by more
+  than half, and in one study a sensitivity and false-positive correction needed fewer human labels than
+  a plain random audit for only 6 of 56 model-verifier pairs on MMLU-Pro and none on MMMU-Pro. 5. Keep
+  the gold sample out of everything it audits: no verifier prompt or threshold tuning, judge fine-tuning
+  or student training on it, and never train the cheap model on verifier rejects or accepted outputs (naive
+  fine-tuning on rejects collapsed the student in every tested setting). 6. Refresh and alert. Re-draw
+  the sample after every student, verifier, prompt or schema change, since the verifier''s blind spot
+  grows as the student gets stronger, and pin the verifier to a dated model snapshot. Keep a frozen human-labeled
+  anchor set that the current verifier re-scores on a schedule, to tell verifier drift from cascade drift:
+  a silent judge version bump was detected in 60 of 60 runs with 200 anchors re-scored every 5th item,
+  but in 4 of 60 with 50 anchors every 20th. Alert when true error diverges from verifier-measured error
+  using a sequential (anytime-valid) test, not a rolling z-test re-run on every new label, which false-alarmed
+  on 75% of drift-free streams; monitor per stratum, because one pooled monitor never detected a drift
+  confined to one stratum. 7. Measure with an A/B of the cascade against the single strong model, both
+  scored on the same gold sample: true error with its interval, cost per item, and escalation rate against
+  the true error rate (a frontier verifier escalated 46% of hard MATH queries against a 39% true error
+  rate in one study).'
