+caveats: The audit practice still rests mainly on one study on GSM8K and MATH (Rajput et al.) and one
+  production text-to-SQL audit whose gold came from two of its authors (234 enriched and 200 random cases);
+  neither compares a cascade run with an audit against one without. The label-count results come from
+  multiple-choice benchmarks with exact answer keys (simulated audits on MMLU-Pro and MMMU-Pro; MMLU and
+  MT-Bench for the factor-two bound) and assume clean, IID gold, while real gold is noisy (annotator kappa
+  0.87; 21.7-25.5% candidate errors in one expert-written benchmark) and rare failures need far larger
+  samples. The 6-of-56 result is an idealized comparison for one correction method; prediction-powered
+  estimators with a tuned weight are never worse than the gold labels alone, so verifier labels can still
+  tighten an interval, just not by much. The drift-monitor results come from one author, two datasets
+  and one model family as judge, with a strong LLM judge rather than humans scoring the main stream.
