+summary: 'Choose the agent''s action interface per model and task: typed tools, a shell, or code that
+  calls tools. Weaker models do better with typed tools (read, write, edit, grep, a narrow calculator):
+  a general code interpreter made a weak agent write boilerplate and fail until it was replaced with typed
+  functions, and structured data gets a typed query tool whatever the model (SQL scored 100% against 52.7%
+  for bash at a seventh of the tokens; SQL plus bash also reached 100%). Strong models on shell-heavy
+  or general office work get bash (on enterprise digital-worker tasks bash-only beat typed tools by 21.8-24.5
+  points with fewer tokens, and typed tools on top of bash added nothing), and work that chains many calls
+  or processes bulk data gets code that calls the tools, because intermediate results never pass through
+  the model: it matched or beat JSON calling for 11 of 14 models on BFCL v4, beat one-at-a-time calls
+  on a many-call benchmark for all ten models tested and for small open models from 4B parameters up,
+  but not at 2B or below, and a narrow calculator did as well as full code execution on math. The best
+  choice is specific to the model and task (two frontier coding agents'' pass rates moved under 3 points
+  across interfaces while cost moved by up to 24.6%, and the same actions offered as typed calls or as
+  command text moved small models'' scores by up to 36.6 points in either direction), so start minimal,
+  add tools per observed failure, provision packages, interpreter state and concurrent commands for code
+  and shell, sandbox it (10.a.i), and decide by a paired eval per model.'
+how: '1. Start from the baseline: the current interface and model. Log per task success, consistency over
+  repeated runs (pass^k), cost, steps, wall-clock time, errors by kind (tools that do not exist, malformed
+  commands, broken edits, import or command-not-found errors) and which tools were actually called. Enforce
+  the interface in configuration where you can and audit the calls: in one study a restriction set only
+  in the prompt still let 7 and 4 explicit edit-tool calls through in 110 runs, and could not rule out
+  the same edits made through bash. 2. Probe each model on 20 or more representative cases under each
+  candidate interface (typed tools only, bash only, typed tools plus bash, code that calls the tools),
+  with the same prompt and budget and several repeats. Do not assume the direction: the same actions offered
+  as typed calls or as command text moved small models'' scores by up to 36.6 points, and typed calls
+  won in only 8 of 18 model-environment pairs. Signs that a model needs typed tools: frequent environment
+  errors under bash (3.11 per trajectory for a weak coding model against 0.35-0.54 for stronger ones;
+  typed tools cut its malformed commands from 0.96 to 0.01 per trajectory), bash-only runs that end after
+  emitting tools that do not exist (66% of one weak model''s runs), or a model of 2B parameters or fewer,
+  where code that calls tools scored slightly below typed calls. 3. Pick the interface per task type.
+  Structured data: a typed query tool (SQL 100% against 52.7% for bash), with bash beside it if the agent
+  should check its own answers. Shell-heavy coding or office work with a strong model: bash (typed tools
+  on top of bash gave no gain on enterprise tasks and raised input tokens 20% for two strong coding models).
+  Many dependent calls, loops, filters, joins or bulk data: code that calls the tools as typed functions
+  and returns only the result (GPT-5.4 went from 18.6% to 71.5% on one many-call benchmark; 37% fewer
+  tokens in Anthropic''s test). Repository edits: measure before dropping native edit tools (code-only
+  cost 14.4% more with Claude Code, not significant, with 39.9% more output tokens, but 19.9% less with
+  Codex). Where success ties, as it did for two frontier coding agents (pass rates within 3 points across
+  default tools, bash only and code only), choose on cost (code-only cost 24.6% less on computation tasks
+  with Claude Code). 4. Start minimal (bash, read, write, edit, grep, or one code-execution tool) and
+  add a tool only when a trace shows a failure it would have prevented. 5. Provision code and shell: install
+  the packages and fixtures the tasks expect; keep interpreter state between code calls (persistent cells
+  raised pass rate in all eight matched comparisons by 0.6-5.7 points, several within run-to-run variation,
+  and cut follow-up API calls from about 27-30 to 4-8); let the code load tool schemas on demand rather
+  than all up front (most of the token savings in that study); allow concurrent commands; and sandbox
+  it (10.a.i). 6. Measure: a paired eval per model and task type, interface A against B with the same
+  model, prompt and budget and several repeats, comparing success and pass^k, cost, steps, wall-clock
+  time and the share of calls made through the assigned interface; re-run it when the model changes.'
