-  the table in the summary. 3. Paired eval, retriever A against B, on the agent's own corpus, measuring
-  evidence localization as well as document recall.
+summary: 'Match the retriever to the corpus: let the agent grep and read the raw files while the corpus
+  is bounded, and put a retriever in front of grep once it grows. On about 100K long web documents, grep
+  and file reads beat an 8B embedding retriever (80.0% against 69.0%) at 29% lower cost, but grep over
+  the whole corpus fell to 37.5% and 32% of questions at 400K documents in two studies, while an agent
+  that pulled retrieved candidates into a working folder and grepped there held 80 to 70 of 100 from 100K
+  to 10M documents at a fraction of the time and cost. The index behind it matters less than older baselines
+  suggested: dense beat default, shallow BM25, but BM25 tuned for long documents and retrieved 1,000 deep
+  matched the dense agent, and on code, embedding search nearly doubled an 8B model''s localization over
+  grep but added 3.7 points at 32B. Use a knowledge-graph retriever only for multi-hop and synthesis questions,
+  routed per query; keep chunk retrieval for single-fact lookup, return source text with any graph triplets,
+  and avoid global community-summary search for detail questions.'
+how: '1. Start from the baseline: record the retriever the agent uses today, the corpus size in documents
+  and average document length, how often it changes, and a sample of real questions labeled single-fact
+  lookup, multi-hop or multi-constraint, synthesis, or code localization; run the current agent on that
+  sample and keep its accuracy, tool calls, wall time and cost. 2. For a bounded corpus (up to about 100K
+  long documents, or single documents of a few hundred thousand tokens), store one file per document in
+  a folder and give the agent grep (ripgrep) plus line-ranged file reads instead of an embedding retriever
+  or the whole corpus in context. On about 100K web documents this scored 80.0% against 69.0% for an 8B
+  embedding retriever at 29% lower cost, and with a small model 61% against 45% (embeddings) and 32% (BM25);
+  a coding agent with no retriever scored 88.5% against 72.5% for an embedding-search agent, one file
+  per document beat one JSON file (89.0 against 83.0 on 100 questions), and on 385K to 536K-token documents
+  it beat GPT-5 reading the text in 200K-token windows by 11.3 and 12.5 points (on 188K-token documents
+  the two tied, 61.5 against 61.0). Allowing piped shell commands raised accuracy from 61% to 73% but
+  cost 2.9 times as much per question. 3. As the corpus grows into the hundreds of thousands of long documents,
+  stop running grep over all of it: expose a retriever as a tool that pulls a few hundred ranked candidates
+  into a working folder, show a short ranked preview, and let the agent grep and read across that folder.
+  Grep alone fell from 67 to 32 and 29 of 100 questions at 100K, 400K and 800K documents as tool errors
+  rose from 1.6% to 54.7%, while the pulled workspace held 80 to 70 of 100 from 100K to 10M documents
+  at $4.03 to $5.17 per 100 questions; on all 830 questions it scored 71.2% against 62.9% at 146 s against
+  3,139 s and $34.91 against $88.13. Keep cross-document grep inside the workspace: blocking it dropped
+  accuracy from 82 to 40 of 100. Corpora of short passages tolerate grep further: over 21M Wikipedia passages
+  grep-based agents still averaged 83.0 in one study, and in another a coding agent with no retriever
+  beat BM25 RAG on Natural Questions (56.0 against 47.0 exact match). 4. Choose and tune the index behind
+  that tool, and test before adding one beside grep. A dense embedder beat default BM25 returning the
+  top 5 (gpt-5: 55.9% to 70.1%), and bigger embedders found more evidence (0.6B to 8B: recall@100 26.5
+  to 47.7), but BM25 tuned for long documents (k1 25, b 1 against the default 0.9, 0.4) and retrieved
+  1,000 deep behind a paged, cached ranking reached 74.6% with gpt-5 against 73.0% for the published dense
+  agent (a different harness) and 83.1% with gpt-5.5; inside the same workspace dense led BM25 by 82 to
+  80 of 100. Adding a retriever to an agent that already greps did not reliably help (88.5% with none,
+  84.0% with embeddings, 78.5% with BM25 in one study), and the agent grepped less when it had one. 5.
+  For coding agents, start with grep, glob and file reads, and add embedding search or a code graph where
+  the model is small or the repository large. Semantic search beside grep raised offline accuracy 12.5%
+  on average (6.5 to 23.5% by model) and code retention 2.6% on 1,000+ file repositories in one vendor''s
+  tests; on SWE-bench Verified localization, embedding search nearly doubled grep-only function accuracy
+  for an 8B open model (16.7% to 30.3%) but added 3.7 points at 32B (32.0% to 35.7%), where the model
+  called it in a third of episodes. Graph tools helped mainly by looking up named functions and expanding
+  one hop. Keep any code index fresh: none of eleven open coding-agent harnesses studied uses embeddings
+  for code retrieval, and the study''s authors point to embeddings going stale as code changes. 6. Add
+  a knowledge-graph retriever only for multi-hop and synthesis questions, routed per query, and keep chunk
+  retrieval for single-fact lookup (plain RAG led on single-hop NQ, F1 64.78 against 63.01 for the best
+  graph method). Return source text with any triplets (F1 50.27 against 34.28 for triplets alone) and
+  avoid global community-summary search for detail questions (null-query accuracy 19.27 against 96.01).
+  Routing gained 1.1% over the best single path and concatenating both 6.4% on one benchmark, while graph
+  construction took 5,560 to 7,702 s against 135 s for chunk RAG. A builder trained with RL to reorganize
+  documents into a linked workspace beat embedding and reranker retrieval for small answering agents (0.75
+  against 0.44 at best), but the same builder untrained scored 0.28, below GraphRAG''s 0.36. 7. Measure
+  with a paired eval, retriever A against B, on the agent''s own corpus and question sample, same model
+  and judge, split by question type and by corpus size (pad the corpus with unrelated documents at 2,
+  4 and 8 times to see where grep breaks): answer accuracy, evidence localization and gold-document recall
+  (surfaced and actually read), tool calls, tool errors and timeouts, wall time and cost per question,
+  and index build and refresh time.'
