-  after.
+summary: 'Limit what the agent can reach at the operating system, not in its prompt or tool wrappers.
+  Run the agent process and every program it starts in one OS-level sandbox (a VM, gVisor or a hardened
+  container) with network denied by default, a path guard that resolves symlinks and checks the file it
+  will actually act on, credentials kept outside the sandbox behind a proxy or scrubbed from the command
+  environment, agent-written SQL read-only with timeouts and row caps, and resource limits with headroom.
+  In one study of 74 scripted attacks on a coding agent, a tool allowlist plus a command sandbox like
+  those deployed agents use let 56 succeed, while confining the whole agent and resolving every request
+  let 2 succeed at the cost of 3 of 46 benign operations; a vendor reports its sandbox cut permission
+  prompts 84% and that only environment controls could have stopped a pasted prompt that exfiltrated credentials
+  in 24 of 25 runs. The limits: secrets still leave through allowed hosts in encoded form, seven frontier
+  models escaped misconfigured containers in every run, and memory limits pinned at a benchmark''s spec
+  failed 5.8% of its runs.'
+how: '1. Start from the baseline: list everything the agent and the programs it starts can reach (files
+  it can read and write, including ~/.ssh, ~/.aws and .env; network destinations; credentials in its environment
+  or in readable files; database roles; local sockets and daemons; and files other tools execute later,
+  such as editor tasks, agent hooks, git config and HEAD, and virtual-environment interpreters), then
+  run out-of-bounds probes against the current setup and record which succeed. 2. Isolate at the operating
+  system, not in the tool wrapper: run the agent process itself and every program it starts, interpreters
+  included, under one policy (in one study a tool allowlist plus a command sandbox that confined only
+  spawned programs still let 56 of 74 attacks through; also confining the agent process and resolving
+  every request to the object it acts on let 2 through). Use a VM or gVisor where the agent runs unattended
+  or its users cannot judge shell commands, and prefer maintained primitives (hypervisors, gVisor, seccomp,
+  bubblewrap, Seatbelt) over code you write yourself. In containers, never use privileged mode, a mounted
+  Docker socket or writable host mounts, drop unneeded capabilities, and patch the kernel and runtime:
+  seven frontier models escaped such misconfigured containers in every run. Remove any switch the agent
+  can use to run a command outside the sandbox, and do not load project-local config or hooks before the
+  user trusts the folder. 3. Deny network by default and route allowed traffic through a proxy outside
+  the sandbox. Treat each allowlisted host as a capability grant, not a destination filter: an allowlisted
+  API can accept uploads to an attacker''s account, so at the proxy pass only requests that carry the
+  session''s own token, bind entries to host and port, and resolve host names so a name that points at
+  an internal address is refused. 4. Bound files by object, not by name: allow writes only inside the
+  workspace and deny reads where secrets live; resolve every path (.., symlinks, hard links, case aliases)
