+  instructions, and keep personal data out of the model''s context. Tokenize PII in the client, wrap untrusted
+  results in source-labeled delimiters, scan context files, skills and tool outputs for injection, and
+  state which source wins a conflict. These are cheap layers against opportunistic attacks, not a boundary:
+  in one study an adaptive attacker reached 99% attack success against delimiter-based spotlighting on
+  Gemini 2.5 Pro and 90 to 94% against three injection detectors, and in another none of 14 open-weight
+  models reliably kept even two declared priority tiers in order. Choose a detector on your agent''s own
+  tool outputs at a low false-positive rate (on AgentDojo, PIGuard, which caught 95.1% of injections on
+  BIPIA, caught 2.1% at 1% false positives, while two agent-oriented detectors caught 73.8 and 82.2%),
+  scan repository docs first (GPT-5.4 followed an injection in a README or TODO file 2.6 times as often
+  as in code or data files), and put consequential actions behind containment that does not depend on
+  spotting the attack (6.c.i, 6.c.ii, 10.a.i).'
+how: '1. Start from a baseline: list every place personal data and untrusted text enter the context (tool
+  and MCP results, web pages, email, repository docs such as README and TODO files, context files such
+  as AGENTS.md, skills, tool descriptions, memory), then measure the current agent: plant a canary instruction
+  at the start, middle and end of each surface and record attack success, how failed attacks fail, and
+  task success with and without the attack. Weight the surfaces by risk: in one study with a fixed payload,
+  GPT-5.4 followed it in 86% of runs that read it from a README against 19% from a code file (reconnaissance
+  goal), 2.6 times as often on average in README or TODO files as in code or data files, and placing it
+  at the end of the content was most effective in all three domains tested. Do not read a low attack rate
+  as refusal: 66.8% of GPT-5.4''s and 81.3% of GPT-4o''s failed injections were ignored, not flagged.
+  2. Keep sensitive data out of the model: tokenize PII in the client or MCP layer so the model handles
+  placeholders while real values flow from tool to tool (Anthropic''s design, unmeasured), and drop untrusted
+  segments from the context once the step that needed them is done. 3. Mark untrusted content: wrap tool,
+  web and repository-derived text in delimiters that name its source, and defang lookalike closing tags
+  inside it (as the Hermes and OpenHands harnesses do). Expect little from this alone: in one AgentDojo
+  study, spotlighting moved static attack success from 30% to 28% on Gemini 2.5 Pro and from 35% to 28%
+  on Llama 3.3 70B; utility held on Gemini 2.5 Pro (74.23% to 75.26%) but fell from 56.70% to 39.18% on
+  Llama 3.3 70B, so measure utility on your model. 4. State a precedence order (operator policy, then
+  user, then repository configuration, with tool and external content as data), then test where it holds
+  on your model instead of assuming the system message protects it. In one study of 14 open-weight models,
+  the data ruled out that any model kept even two declared priority tiers in order in 95% of replies;
+  the first of two conflicting lines in one message won 72.42 to 94.66% of decided replies; and in tool
