-summary: Track repeated identical actions (same tool, same arguments; same search query) and turns with
-  no new information. Inject a warning at a small streak (about 5 identical calls), refuse exact-repeat
-  queries, and end the run early at a longer streak (about 8 identical failures) or when progress has
-  stalled, instead of running to the turn cap. A prompt line telling the agent not to repeat itself is
-  a weak version of the same thing and is not a substitute.
-how: 1. In the loop, hash each tool call (name plus arguments) and each query; keep the last N. 2. On
-  a streak of 5, inject a 'you are repeating; change approach' message; on 8 identical failures, stop
-  with the best answer so far. 3. Define 'stalled' for the task (no new file, result or fact in K turns)
-  and stop on it. 4. Measure loop and turn-cap failures before and after.
+summary: 'When an agent repeats the same call and gets the same result, push it to change approach after
+  a few repeats, and end the run only after a long streak of identical failures. It pays mainly for loop-prone
+  models: loops appeared in 5.21% of Meta''s production coding-agent trajectories, where an observer that
+  flagged them (with other misbehaviors) and injected course-correction reminders cut tokens per session
+  5.3%, and on OSWorld forcing a change of approach after repeated no-change actions added up to 4.24
+  points for Gemini 3 Flash but 0.04 for Claude Sonnet 4.6 at 100 steps, and cost 6.15 points at a 15-step
+  budget. A prompt line or a silent refusal of exact repeats is not a substitute (with repeats refused,
+  an agent proposed already-run calls as often as before), and repetition alone is not failure: identical
+  repeats were rare after tool faults, retries with changed arguments were common, and blind retries were
+  as frequent in passing coding runs as in failing ones. Treat the stop as a tested backstop, not a saving:
+  ending stuck runs cost a small model 3.4 points of success, a rule-based abandon never fired for a frontier
+  terminal agent, and advisory controllers raised cost 19% to 47% with no gain, so test any controller
+  end to end against plain rules and a placebo.'
+how: '1. Start from the baseline: on the eval set or recorded traces, per model, measure the share of
+  runs with a loop (the same normalized call, tool name plus arguments with whitespace and argument order
+  normalized, returning the same result as an earlier call), the share of steps spent in loops, runs ending
+  at the turn cap or deadline, success, tokens, turns and wall-clock. Expect loops in a minority of runs
+  that varies by model and setup: 5.21% of a five-week sample of one company''s production coding-agent
+  trajectories (2.18% for Claude Sonnet 4.5, 3.59% for Claude Opus 4.5), 12.1% to 20.7% of failed OSWorld
+  tasks for two current backbones, and 21.3% to 40.0% of CodeGym tasks for three Gemini models with full
+  history (any single repeat counted), more once history is summarized (5.a.i). 2. Compare the call together
+  with its result, not the call alone: keep a hash of each normalized call and of its result (for a GUI
+  agent, of the screen) over a recent window, and count a repeat only when the same call returns the same
+  result or the same state recurs with no visible change; normalize search queries the same way to catch
+  near-duplicates. A streak of calls to one tool with changing arguments is usually a retry, not a loop:
+  after injected tool faults, agents called the same tool three or more times in a row in 12.5% to 22.2%
+  of trials but with identical arguments in only 0.8% to 1.8%, and on SWE-bench Verified blind retries
+  appeared in 46.0% of passing and 44.9% of failing runs (regression loops 38.7% and 39.5%), while cyclic
+  patterns were more common in failing runs (44.5% against 33.6%) and wasted more steps there (7.8 against
+  4.6 per instance). 3. On a short streak, inject a message that names the repeated call and its count
+  and requires a different approach, and escalate if the streak continues. Published harnesses warn at
+  two to five identical calls (one study''s harness at five identical calls or five identical failures,
+  Hermes after two identical failures; OpenCode asks the user at three). In a desktop agent that forced
+  a different interaction mode after two identical actions with no visible change and a new strategy after
+  the same screen recurred three times, steps spent in loops fell from 4.9% to 2.8% (Gemini 3 Flash) and
+  from 3.2% to 2.1% (Claude Sonnet 4.6); success rose 1.82 to 4.24 points for Gemini 3 Flash at 50 and
+  100 steps and 0.04 to 1.44 for Sonnet 4.6, but fell 6.15 points at a 15-step budget, where the escalation
+  steps crowded out the task. In production, 94.29% of loops flagged by an asynchronous observer did not
+  recur after one injected course correction (judged by an LLM). Neither a prompt line nor a silent refusal
+  changes behavior on its own: refusing an exact repeat and returning the earlier result kept repeated
+  calls from running (5.7% of tasks ran one, 46.7% tried) but the agent proposed already-run calls as
+  often as before (71.3% of tasks against 69.8%) and used as many tokens (24.9k against 23.9k), so keep
+  refusal only to avoid re-running calls with side effects (2.b.i); on that small model a reminder when
+  stuck left steps (14.85 against 15.00) and success (23.7% against 25.1%) about where they were. 4. Keep
+  a stop rule as a backstop, not a saving: end the run at a long streak of identical failures (eight in
+  two published harnesses, one of which ships the stop disabled) or when the task''s progress signal (step
+  5) has been flat for K turns, and return the best answer so far with the stop reason (9.b.i). Expect
+  it to fire rarely and to cost successes when it fires early: on a small open model under aggressive
+  summarization, ending the run when a stuck detector fired cut steps from 15.00 to 13.08 and tokens from
+  23.9k to 21.6k per task but success fell from 25.1% to 21.7% (one run), and a rule to abandon after
+  four consecutive failures past 40% of the step budget never fired in 63 runs of a Claude Sonnet 5.5
+  terminal agent (without a controller, 10 of 17 failures ended at a timeout or cap instead). Repetition
+  alone does not show a task is infeasible: require separate evidence before reporting failure. 5. Define
+  ''no progress'' for the task from what the run produces, not from its length: no new file or diff, test
+  result, retrieved fact, screen state or change to the draft answer in K turns; the tool-step count alone
+  ranked failing coding runs at chance (AUC 0.471 to 0.528). Put the detector in place before raising
+  a turn cap, keep the cap and deadline as the hard bound (7.b.iii), track progress items explicitly where
+  the task allows (7.b.iv), and make every stop path, detector or cap, return an extractable answer (9.b.i).
+  6. Keep detection cheap and test anything smarter end to end. Hashes and counters cost nothing; an LLM
+  check after every action does not: a loop breaker whose top tier asked an LLM judge after each action
+  cut actions per task from 16.80 to 14.28 but raised model calls from 28.40 to at least 41.52 and tracked
+  tokens from at least 257,618 to at least 422,074, and the production observer ran its LLM classifier
+  asynchronously at fixed intervals so the agent never waited on it. Compare any added controller against
+  plain rules and a placebo: on a terminal benchmark, advisory messages at small failure and repetition
+  streaks plus verify prompts at finish raised cost per attempt 24% (rules), 19% (a cheap decision model)
+  and 47% (a small LLM) with no detectable pass-rate change, while random advice at the same moments did
+  not raise cost (0.98 times). Don''t add a learned stop monitor yet: one trained on past runs saved 4.5%
+  of cost per retained solve at 99.0% retention for one agent, catching 5.0% of failing runs, and nothing
+  for another, and stopping pays only when the execution it avoids exceeds the monitor''s cost plus the
+  successes it kills. 7. Measure on the same cases and model, at the step budgets you run, with several
+  runs per arm: no detector, warning only, warning plus stop (and a placebo reminder when testing a controller);
+  report the share of runs with a loop and of steps spent in loops, warnings issued and whether the repeated
+  call recurred after them, stops and cap hits by reason, success overall and on the runs the stop ended
+  (replay them without the stop to count the successes it cut), tokens, turns and wall-clock. Re-measure
+  when the model changes: in one production sample the newer model looped more (3.59% against 2.18%).'
