+caveats: 'Most of the measured evidence is small, bundled or from single runs: the desktop study reports
+  single point estimates with fixed, unswept thresholds and an LLM judge inside its loop breaker; the
+  production A/B test bundles loops with other misbehaviors and judges recovery with an LLM; the stuck-stop,
+  reminder and blocker controls ran once on one benchmark with an 8B open model under aggressive 4,096-token
+  summarization; and VeRO''s items come from bundled optimizer runs. Stopping is the least supported part:
+  ending stuck runs lost 3.4 points on that small model, a rule-based abandon never fired for a frontier
+  terminal agent across 63 runs, advisory controllers raised cost with no gain on 21 tasks, and a learned
+  stop monitor saved 4.5% for one agent and nothing for another, so treat the stop as a backstop to test.
+  Repetition is not failure: identical repeats were rare after tool faults while retries with changed
+  arguments were common, blind retries were as frequent in passing coding runs as in failing ones, and
+  the one fix that removed most loops in the summarization study was training on the agent''s own loops
+  (57.3% to 6.1%), not a harness rule. Gains depend on model and budget, and newer is not safer: a loop-prone
+  model gained up to 4.24 points from forced changes where a strong one gained 0.04 at 100 steps, and
+  in production Claude Opus 4.5 looped more often than Claude Sonnet 4.5 (3.59% against 2.18%).'
