-summary: 'A separate evaluator (an LLM judge with written criteria, a different model, or an agent that
-  tests the running app) scores the generator''s output and returns specific findings; the generator revises
-  until the output passes or a round cap is hit. It pays when the task is beyond what the generator does
-  reliably in one pass and the evaluator is more reliable than the generator: a production diff judge
-  vetoed about a quarter of coding-agent sessions, and half of those recovered. It fails when the judge
-  is weak or shares the generator''s blind spots: judge loops left table extraction worse than the first
-  output and raised essay scores while human scores fell. Most of the gain comes in the first two to four
-  rounds, at roughly twice the tokens of one call.'
-how: '1. Start from the single pass, tuned with the same effort, and from best-of-N at equal tokens; add
-  the loop only where outputs often fall short and no compiler, test or execution check can judge them
-  (if one exists, feed its verdict back instead, 9.a.i). 2. Write the evaluator''s criteria as a few gradable
-  items, each with a hard pass threshold, and calibrate it with a few scored examples; where possible
-  let it check by acting (drive the app with Playwright, compare the diff with the original request).
-  The same criteria in the generator''s prompt also steer the first draft. 3. Make the evaluator separate
-  and skeptical: its own call and context, not the generator''s dialogue history; a model at least as
-  capable as the generator; tune its prompt by reading its logs against your own verdicts until it stops
-  approving problems it found. Check it on labeled good and bad outputs and planted-defect probes before
-  wiring it in. A judge from another model family does not remove favoritism (judges favor any model whose
-  mistakes resemble theirs), and content-free answers such as '':'' or ''Thought process:'' can pass it, so
-  include them among the probes. 4. Return findings the generator can act on (failing criterion or checklist
-  item, where, expected against actual; told which checklist items failed, Gemini 2.5 Pro went from 29.5
-  to 94.7, against -2.5 to +2.6 with no feedback) and tell it to keep what already passed; unconstrained
-  regeneration breaks parts that were right.
-  5. Stop on pass or after a low cap (most gains came in the first 2 to 4 rounds); raise the cap only
-  if the per-round curve keeps rising. Return the best version rather than the last, and stop early when
-  the score stalls or drops. 6. Measure against a check the loop never sees (a human-labeled sample or
-  a deterministic metric), per round: quality, share of items that end worse than their first output,
-  evaluator agreement with the audit, tokens and latency.'
+summary: 'Have a separate, skeptical evaluator check each output against written criteria and send back
+  specific findings, revising until it passes or a low round cap. It pays when the task is beyond what
+  the generator does reliably in one pass and the evaluator is at least as strong and sees every requirement:
+  a production diff judge vetoed about a quarter of coding-agent sessions and half of those recovered,
+  and a strong grader lifted a weak writer''s projects more than the writer''s own grading did. On strong
+  current models most of the gain is extra budget: given the same rounds without feedback, Opus 5.5 and
+  GPT-6 Sol came within a point of the loop, and at equal calls best-of-four sampling matched or beat
+  a reviewer loop in 13 of 16 code-quality comparisons. It backfires when the evaluator is weak, blind
+  to the requirements or gamed: feedback from a judge that could not see the instructions left outputs
+  worse than no feedback and worse each round, judge loops degraded table extraction and inflated essay
+  scores, and attackers revised code to a reviewer''s findings to get vulnerable changes approved.'
+how: '1. Start from three baselines: the single pass tuned with the same effort, best-of-N at equal calls,
+  and the same model given the same extra rounds or budget with no feedback. Add the loop only where outputs
+  often fall short and no compiler, test or execution check can judge them (if one exists, feed its verdict
+  back instead, 9.a.i). On strong current models the baselines take most of the gain: with seven models
+  as writers and graders of data science projects, Opus 5.5 and GPT-6 Sol scored 94% and 93% after two
+  rounds of feedback against 93% and 92% revising twice without it, and at four calls best-of-four ranked
+  by a static analyzer matched or beat a coder-reviewer loop in 13 of 16 code-quality comparisons. The
+  loop paid for weaker writers (GPT-6 Luna 78% to 87%) and where the writer had broken a rule without
+  knowing it (Haiku 4.5, 8% to 45%, once told its data were generated, not real). 2. Write the evaluator''s
+  criteria as a few gradable items, each with a hard pass threshold, and calibrate it with a few scored
+  examples; where possible let it check by acting (drive the app with Playwright, run the code and read
+  the result, compare the diff with the original request). Show it everything the output must respect:
+  the request, the system prompt or policy, and the source the output draws on. In one study, revising
+  on reports from a judge shown only the response cut instruction constraints met from 84% to 70% in one
+  round while a revise-only control rose to 87%, and it fell again each round; a judge that saw the request
+  without its separately stated constraints still lost 8 points. The same criteria in the generator''s
+  prompt also steer the first draft. 3. Make the evaluator separate and skeptical: its own call and context,
+  not the generator''s dialogue history, and a model at least as capable as the generator. A stronger
+  grader lifted a weak writer (Haiku 4.5''s final projects scored 45% by other graders when Opus 5.5 graded
+  the rounds, 36% when it graded itself), weaker graders did not improve stronger writers, and models
+  grading their own work scored it about 9 points higher than other graders did. Tune its prompt by reading
+  its logs against your own verdicts until it stops approving problems it found. Check it on labeled good
