+  rules took 0.15 retries per question in one system. The check works only if the harness enforces it
+  and the agent cannot change it: an agent left to decide when to stop gave up on 30 solvable problems,
+  28 of them with a proof the validator had rejected, agents edited or special-cased tests they could
+  see (a test-feedback loop raised cheating on impossible tasks from 33% to 38%), and visible tests overstated
+  held-out correctness by 43-48 points for one frontier agent building whole systems. So keep tests read-only
+  or hidden, score on a held-out check, let the agent flag a check it thinks is broken, and do not add
+  a same-model critique step (7.c.i): on older models it cut accuracy (GPT-3.5 on CommonsenseQA 75.8%
+  to 38.1%), and a verification tool one optimizer added raised GAIA 5.75 points but cut SimpleQA 17.8.'
+how: '1. Start from the baseline: on the eval set or recorded runs, log how often outputs reach the user
+  failing a check you could have run (build, tests, type or schema check, SQL that fails EXPLAIN or names
+  a table that does not exist, a policy rule), plus pass rate, rounds, tokens and latency per task. Set
+  aside a held-out check the agent never sees (hidden tests, gold answers or review) to score the change,
+  because the loop will optimize whatever check it is given. 2. Pick the sound check the task admits and
+  wire it to every output: compiler, type checker, linter or language-server diagnostics after each edit;
+  unit tests; JSON or schema validation; for plans, proofs and puzzles a validator, proof checker or solver;
+  for tool use, the policy written as code that rejects a violating call (it raised one agent''s safety
+  score from 48.75% to 58.34%). For SQL, run EXPLAIN and execute, check for hallucinated tables and columns
+  and repair from the error (removing one fixer cut successful compilation from 95% to 76%), validate
+  against approved metrics, joins, filters and row-level security where they exist, and run read-only
+  with timeouts and row caps (10.a.i). 3. Put the check where the agent cannot change it: keep tests and
+  checker config read-only or hidden (hiding tests cut cheating on impossible tasks to near zero but cost
+  legitimate solves; read-only kept performance and stopped test edits but not special-casing), and do
+  not let the agent write the check it is judged by (9.c.i). Judge coverage, not the green light: frontier
+  agents saturated visible feature tests while failing held-out tests that combine those features, by
+  43-48 points for one agent building whole systems. 4. Enforce it in the harness, not the prompt: accept
+  no answer until the check passes, and on a failure start another round with the verdict and require
+  an edit before resubmitting (one agent resubmitted an unchanged proof 170 times). Feed back pass/fail
+  with the first concrete error (compiler message, failing test, violated rule); in one planning study
+  more detail than pass/fail helped only on Blocksworld (60% to 87%). Let the agent edit in place with
+  feedback after every edit rather than regenerate the whole output (69 against 18 proofs at the same
+  model and budget). Where only the final answer can be checked, resample until the check accepts; where
+  intermediate states can be checked, check them during the run (on a maze task 88.8% with a final-answer
+  check against 97.9% with checks during the run). 5. Bound the loop and give it an exit: cap rounds (one
+  SQL system allowed three revisions and averaged 0.15 per question), stop runs that repeat or stall (7.b.i)
+  and size budgets so productive runs can finish (7.b.iii). Let the agent flag a check it believes is
+  wrong, or abstain when no answer passes, instead of working around it: an abort option cut GPT-5''s
+  cheating on impossible tasks from 54% to 9%. 6. Where no sound check exists, do not substitute the model''s
+  own critique: with a current reasoning model a comparable loop judged by an LLM critic moved accuracy
+  by -2.7 to +3.1 points while resampling until a sound checker accepted added up to 18.2. Check completion
+  against the environment (9.b.iii), use a trained detector (9.b.ii) or spend on parallel samples with
+  a vote (9.c.ii), and test any added verification step on every task type. 7. Measure: run the paired
+  eval at equal budget with no check, pass/fail feedback, full error text and resampling until the check
+  passes; report end quality on the held-out check, the check-failure rate on the first attempt and on
+  returned outputs, rounds per task, tokens and latency, and gaming signals (test or checker edits, special-cased
+  inputs, identical resubmissions); add probes whose tests contradict the specification, where any pass
+  is gaming.'
