+summary: 'Tell a SQL-writing agent what its data means, not only what it is called: what each column,
+  code and NULL means and how each metric is computed, with keys and sample values. The meaning does the
+  work: emptying the prose from an otherwise identical context cost 26 to 36 points on hard data-agent
+  tasks across four models in one ablation, a 4 KB semantic document added 17 to 23 points for three frontier
+  models in a vendor study, and keys and join paths added only a few points over descriptive names in
+  a third study. Put a short document in the prompt; when the rules run long, keep an index in the prompt
+  and let the agent read each rule by name, since the same knowledge pasted as a 24,177-character prompt
+  gained much less, and on a second benchmark long business documents helped less than concise rule snippets.
+  Each result rests on one benchmark, and once the schema outgrows a modest share of the window, link
+  it by exploration (8.b.ii).'
+how: '1. Start from the baseline: run your own questions with the bare schema (table and column names,
+  types) and count what the full context would cost in tokens as you would send it (descriptions, code
+  meanings, sample values, rule documents); if the schema alone takes more than a modest share of the
+  window, use 8.b.ii instead. 2. Make every name say what it holds, then add keys: in one factorial study
+  on corrected BIRD questions, replacing anonymous column names with abbreviated ones moved accuracy 18.5
+  to 42.3 points by difficulty, while adding types, primary keys, foreign keys and join paths to a names-only
+  schema moved it 2.6 to 5.5, most on the hardest questions (models up to Gemini 2.5 Flash). Rename or
+  describe cryptic identifiers first and name link columns plainly: with foreign keys declared, the model
+  still joined on canonical key names. 3. Write down what no column name records: what each code means,
+  what a NULL means (in one benchmark a NULL in a fee rule meant ''applies to all values'', not ''unknown''),
+  and how each metric is computed, as a SQL expression where you can, starting from existing manuals and
+  data dictionaries. Most of the gain is here: emptying that prose from an otherwise identical context
+  cost 26 to 36 points on hard DABStep tasks across four models, and BIRD''s per-question hints added
+  16.36 points on BIRD dev for gemini-1.5-pro. 4. Add sample values for the text and code columns that
+  questions filter on: in one study they added 1.76 points overall and 5.52 on challenging BIRD questions,
+  for about 4,772 more tokens per request (mean plus one standard deviation). 5. Choose the delivery by
+  length. A short document goes in the prompt: a 4 KB semantic document added 17 to 23 points for three
+  frontier models in a vendor study, where an agentic platform holding the same knowledge did not significantly
+  beat raw DDL. When the rules run long, keep an index of domains and metrics in the prompt and have the
+  agent read each rule by name, from files or a lookup tool, before it writes SQL: the same knowledge
+  pasted as a 24,177-character prompt trailed the lookups by 13.7 to 44.6 hard-task points and on one
+  model did no better than the bare schema, and on another benchmark all eight systems tested did better
+  with concise expert-written evidence than with the whole business document (21.4% against 15.9% for
+  the best). 6. Keep the static part cache-friendly (3.b.i) and watch the bill: looked-up rules are billed
+  as fresh input (on GPT-5.6, $8.87 against $3.55 of fresh input for the cached prompt, $21.32 against
+  $14.42 in total), though fewer turns and less reasoning made lookups the cheapest per correct answer
+  on three of four models. 7. Measure with paired runs on your own questions, with the production model
+  and a cheaper one: bare schema against the full context and, where it runs long, prompt against index
+  plus lookups. Report execution accuracy split by questions that need a documented rule and those that
+  don''t (the pasted manual led on the easy tasks on all four models), wrong-table, wrong-column, wrong-value
+  and rule-not-applied failures, whether the SQL carries each documented rule, fresh and cached input
+  tokens, turns, and cost per correct answer, over at least two runs (23.4% of verdicts flipped between
+  two near-identical runs in one study).'
