-summary: 'Return every failed action to the model as an error it can read (the call, the cause, a capped
-  trace) instead of swallowing the exception, retrying silently, returning an empty result or deleting
-  the failed attempt, and collapse each error once it is resolved. Failures that surface as explicit errors
-  were recovered from far more often than failures that look like valid output (37 points more on average
-  across nine models on one benchmark), and a linter that shows its errors and rejects the bad edit raised
-  SWE-bench Lite from 15.0% to 18.0% on one model. The limits: in 135M-1.7B models the verbatim failed
-  call made exact repeats more likely (though deleting it was worse), a model''s own unflagged mistakes
-  in its context raise its later error rate, and on HumanEval restarting fresh after 2-3 failed fixes
-  beat continuing; so keep the diagnosis, cap it, and don''t let failures pile up.'
+summary: 'Show the agent every failed action as a marked error it can read, with the next step it can
+  take, instead of hiding it, and collapse the error once it is fixed. Return the call, the cause and
+  a capped trace as an error result rather than swallowing the exception, retrying silently, returning
+  an empty or plausible-looking result or deleting the failed attempt. On one benchmark agents recovered
+  from explicit failures 37 points more often than from silent ones across nine models, a note naming
+  the recovery tools on silently failed results raised completion from 10.9% to 28.1% on four current
+  open-weight models, and one model almost never misreported a failure marked status:error (at most 1.2%)
+  but answered dishonestly in up to 45.3% of failures returned as status:ok. The limits: deleting the
+  failed exchange did only 1.9-3.8 points worse than keeping it across 13 open models, in 135M-1.7B models
+  the verbatim failed call made exact repeats more likely (though deleting it was worse), a model''s own
+  unflagged mistakes raise its later error rate, on HumanEval restarting fresh after 2-3 failed fixes
+  beat continuing, and instructions planted in third-party error text can steer the agent; so keep the
+  diagnosis and the next step, cap it, strip instructions from third-party errors, and don''t let failures
+  pile up.'
 how: '1. Start from the harness as it is and find every place a failure is hidden from the model: exceptions
   swallowed, silent retries, a failed attempt deleted before the retry, a generic "operation failed" in
-  place of the cause, or an empty or default result returned on failure. Inject a few failures (a tool
-  that fails on first use, a silent wrong result, a third-party error message) and record recovery and
-  repeated failed calls. 2. Return every failed action to the model in the next turn as a tool result
-  marked as an error: the call (tool and arguments), the error type, the cause message and the exit code.
-  Never turn a failure into an empty or plausible-looking result; on one benchmark agents recovered from
-  silent failures 37 points less often than from explicit ones. Keeping the loop alive when a tool raises
-  is 2.b.i. 3. Cap what goes in: keep the exception type and message, the frames in the agent''s own code
-  and the last lines of output; write the full trace or log to a file and give the path (2.c.i); make test
-  and lint runs print a few lines, with ERROR and the reason on one line. Write the errors you own so
-  they name the fix, and strip sentences from third-party error text that ask for steps the agent cannot
-  take (a terminal command, a config change, a web page); in one study a one-sentence filter prompt raised
-  recovery on expired credentials from 45% to 82%. 4. Do not retry from a context with the failure deleted:
-  under greedy decoding that was the harness that repeated most. If the agent re-emits a failed call verbatim
-  (measured in small models), replace the earlier verbatim call with a short harness-written note of what
-  failed and why, or refuse exact repeats (7.b.i); keep the diagnosis either way. 5. Drop errors once they
-  are resolved: when a later call succeeds or the tests pass, collapse the old error observation to a
-  one-line stub, keep only the first of a run of identical format errors, and carry only unresolved errors
-  through compaction (5.a.i). Don''t let failures pile up: after 2-3 failed fixes of the same error, restart
-  from the original task (optionally with a one-line note of what failed) or hand off, since non-thinking
-  models make more mistakes as their own uncorrected errors accumulate in context. 6. Measure with paired
-  runs on the same tasks and injected failures, the harness as is against errors kept, capped and dropped
-  once resolved, per model: task success, recovery rate per injected failure by type (the agent''s own
-  call, credentials, permissions, rate limits, silent wrong results), exact and canonical repeat rate
-  of failed calls, runs ending in a loop, steps and tokens. Re-run after a model change: the repetition
-  effect shrank with model size, and thinking models did not self-condition.'
