+summary: 'Start each call site at a low reasoning effort and a model chosen on its own eval, instead of
+  the top setting everywhere: on agentic coding tasks that every effort level solved, xhigh effort cost
+  2.5 to 5.3 times low effort, and on Terminal-Bench every one of eight models lost some tasks its lowest
+  level had solved. Pick the model per role or call site and tune each role''s prompt to its model (a
+  model per role raised one data agent from 81.4% to 91.6% at lower cost; GEPA raised every model tested
+  at lower cost), give explicit effort budgets by query class (e.g. one agent and 3-10 tool calls for
+  simple fact-finding), and where an external check exists, escalate effort only after a verified failure,
+  which matched or beat fixed top effort''s success for all eight models, mostly at lower cost. Choose
+  by measured cost and quality per task, not list price or release date: a model at a fifth of the per-token
+  price cost only 3.2 times less per task and passed 16.4% of tasks against 27.1%, and the newer generation
+  of each of three model families lost recall on one fraud benchmark (by up to 22.2 points), so try a
+  newer model before doubling token budgets but benchmark it on the site''s eval first. Routers that pick
+  model or effort per query or turn often matched but did not beat the best single choice, and a prompt
+  that forces stepwise reasoning is the same lever from the prompt side and hurt on GPQA; prefer the model''s
+  own effort setting.'
+how: '1. From traces, list call sites and their token, cost, latency and error profile; the baseline is
+  the current model and effort at each site. Count realized cost per task (input including cache reads,
+  reasoning and output, calls per task), not list price: in one spreadsheet-agent study Claude Haiku 4.5,
+  at a fifth of GPT-5.6 Sol''s input price, made about 41 calls per session against about 25 and cost
+  only 3.2 times less per task while passing 16.4% against 27.1%. 2. For each site, run a small model
+  grid (cheaper, specialist, effort levels) on the eval slice that exercises it. Sweep effort from the
+  lowest level up rather than max it: in one eight-model sweep, on tasks every level solved, low to xhigh
+  raised mean cost 2.51 to 5.34 times in the GPT-5.6 and Claude Sonnet 5 cases reported for Terminal-Bench
+  and SWE-Bench Pro (up to 42 times across models and benchmarks), largely through longer trajectories
+  (GPT-5.6-sol 14.7 to 28.4 calls), and on Terminal-Bench every model lost some tasks its lowest level
