+summary: 'Fan work out to parallel sub-agents only when it splits into independent parts, and check that
+  one agent given the same budget doesn''t do as well. In a matched-budget study across six benchmarks,
+  multi-agent setups gained 80.8% on decomposable financial analysis but lost 39-70% on sequential planning
+  and 2-15% on SWE-bench Verified (mean change -0.3%), a role-split customer-service team lost to one
+  agent in 12 of 13 settings, and teams cost more (about 15x a chat''s tokens in one vendor''s research
+  system; 1.7-4.3x one agent''s cost on coding tasks) without always being faster (up to 90% less research
+  time, but 1.2-2.5x longer for a coordinated coding team). For code, split only with a lead that sees
+  every requirement and with explicit ownership and isolation: two parallel agents with conflicting requirements
+  passed jointly 16% of the time against 62% for one agent holding both, while a manager that split a
+  repository by its dependencies, gave each worker its own git worktree and merged through tests gained
+  about 6 points for Claude Sonnet 4.5. When delegating, give each sub-agent an objective, output format,
+  tools, boundaries and the files it owns, have it write outputs to storage and return references, and
+  re-verify what it reports.'
+how: '1. Start from one agent and check the task shape before splitting. Split only when the work has
+  many parts that are independent (no part needs another''s result or must follow another''s decisions),
+  share little context and can be checked separately: breadth-first research, one item per entity or file,
+  independent reviews, code modules with clear interfaces. Keep one agent for sequential work, conversations
+  with a single user and tasks it already does well: at a matched budget, multi-agent setups gained 80.8%
+  on decomposable financial analysis but lost 39-70% on sequential planning and 2-15% on SWE-bench Verified,
+  with gains shrinking once the single agent passed about 45%; a role-split customer-service team lost
+  in 12 of 13 settings, including the two where that 45% rule predicted a gain; and decomposing a text-to-SQL
+  question before looking at the data scored below a single agent. Run the single agent first at the budget
+  the team would get, and treat a threshold from a paper as a hint, not a rule. 2. Let one lead that sees
+  the whole task make the split, and give each sub-agent an objective, an output format, its tools and
+  boundaries, and the context it needs (the relevant trace, not a one-line task: vague tasks led research
+  sub-agents to duplicate work). For code, split along the dependency graph: group files with strong or
+  circular dependencies under one worker, list for each task the files it owns and the interfaces it consumes
+  and produces, mark shared files (package init files, configs) off-limits, and settle shared conventions
+  before workers start. Parallel coding agents that each saw only their own requirement passed jointly
+  16% of the time against 62% for one agent holding both; when agents agreed on an owner for the contested
+  code, conflicts fell from 67% to 18% of runs (61% pass against 29%). 3. Isolate writers and integrate
+  through checks. Give each coding worker its own branch and git worktree, merge into the main branch
+  only after the worker''s relevant tests pass, and have the worker that caused a merge conflict resolve
+  it: on paper reproduction, one shared workspace with files assigned by instruction scored 55.5% (single
+  agent 57.2%), separate worktrees 63.3%. Prefer read-only sub-agents (search, review, investigation)
+  wherever the task allows; they cannot make conflicting edits. 4. Have sub-agents write outputs (code,
+  reports, data) to storage and return references, and have the lead check each report against the artifact
+  before building on it (the stop-time check is 9.b.iii). Making delegation required raised the share
+  of planted issues that reviews reported (49.9% to 69.6%) but did not make their coverage claims honest:
+  50.3% of incomplete delegating reviews still explicitly overclaimed. 5. Cap the number of workers and
+  the team''s total budget, and log spend per worker. Start with a few workers (one research system runs
+  3-5 in parallel; one harness defaults to three concurrent children at depth 1): going from 4 to 8 coding
+  workers lowered the pass rate, turns grew from 7.2 for one agent to 27.7 for an orchestrated team, and
+  under a token ceiling 74% of one team''s loss came from runs that exhausted the budget. 6. Measure the
+  team against one agent at the same token or cost budget (and, where you can, one agent running the same
+  plan-and-review steps in one context), with several runs per arm on your own task mix split by task
+  shape: success, tokens and cost, wall-clock, conflict and rework rate (merge conflicts, overwritten
+  or redone work, parts that pass alone but fail together) and sub-agent reports later found wrong. Do
+  not assume parallel means faster: parallel sub-agents with parallel tool calls cut research time by
+  up to 90%, but a coordinated coding team ran 1.2-2.5x longer than one agent because its merges were
+  sequential and test-gated. Do not run one agent first and fall back to the team after it fails: in one
+  study that nearly added the two runtimes and costs.'
