+summary: 'Split a cleanly decomposable task into a fixed chain of model calls with a code check between
+  steps, and rerun only the step that fails. Give each call one goal; gate with a schema check, an assertion
+  or a run against the real system, and use an LLM check only for subjective criteria. The trade is latency
+  and tokens for accuracy and inspectable intermediate outputs, and it pays only on tasks beyond what
+  one call does reliably: on coding tasks a current strong model mostly solved alone, a three-call chain
+  cost 3.2 times as much and took 3.2 times as long for no gain, while on recurring tasks a fixed program
+  was far cheaper and more consistent than an agent loop. Where a chain beat one call, the gain came mostly
+  from the split and its focused prompts rather than from the gate (one study, small models), and gates
+  misfire: 37% of halts were false alarms in one study, and about half of the failures on markdown-fenced
+  JSON in another were valid once the fence was stripped.'
+how: '1. Start from a single call tuned with the same effort on the same model, and for a recurring task
+  also from the agent loop; chain only if the task splits cleanly into fixed steps and the single call
+  fails on it. On coding tasks a current strong model mostly solved alone, a planner, coder and reviewer
+  chain cost 3.2 times as much, took 3.2 times as long and solved no more; a cheap model in the planner
+  and reviewer roles left it dearer, slower and 8.1 points less accurate than the single call, so to save
+  cost choose the model per task first (1.b.i). Against an agent loop on recurring analytics tasks, a
+  fixed program with model calls only at judgment points used about 70 times fewer input tokens and a
+  third of the runtime, and was correct on every run. 2. Give each step one goal and a typed handoff the
+  next step can validate against a schema, and validate it right after the call that produced it, before
+  the next call: in one study an unvalidated intermediate failed only at final assembly, after the next
+  call had already run. 3. Gate in code first: schema validation or assertions; where you can, run the
+  output against the real system (execute the query) instead of asking the model whether it is valid;
+  use an LLM check with hard thresholds only for subjective criteria. Strip format wrappers before validating
+  and record what remains: in one study 48.0% of failures on markdown-fenced JSON validated once the fence
+  was removed. 4. On a failure, rerun only the failed step with its previous output and the specific violation,
+  not a generic parse error: in that study all 24 of Claude Haiku 4.5''s steps that exhausted their retries
+  ended on a length cap, and the parse error returned for their fenced attempts never named it. Cap retries (up to three further attempts completed 948 of
+  973 runs there), and never rerun the whole chain on a deterministic validation error (six calls per
+  injected fault, then a silently dropped item, in another study). 5. When retries run out, stop or continue
+  with a warning, keep outputs that already passed, and record the failure. 6. Run new gates in warn-only
+  mode first and review each halt for false alarms. 7. Log each step''s output, gate result and attempts;
+  compare the chain with the single call (and with the agent loop where it is the alternative) on success,
+  tokens, cost per solved task and latency, per model.'
