+  step, until it answers or hits a cap. It pays where the agent has to find its own path and recover from
+  wrong steps: with the same lightweight models, each model''s best loop harness resolved 51 to 63 of
+  100 SWE-bench tasks against 17 to 39 for a fixed repair pipeline (the loops used 4.3 to 87.8 million
+  tokens per resolved task against 0.1 to 0.2 million), and on goal-only Google Maps tasks generic loops
+  that kept the full history beat a fixed-step loop by 9 to 28 points. Where the lookups or steps are
+  known in advance it buys little: a one-decision pipeline matched a tool loop on banking cases (90.5%
+  against 88.8% correct), a fixed-step loop matched generic ones on stepwise tests at 2.6 to 10.7 times
+  lower cost, and on web tasks with a trusted API, compiling a program before reading any page beat ReAct
+  by 11.1 points and kept injected content from steering its steps, at 2.0 to 4.4 times the cost. On current
+  models use native tool calling, carry the model''s reasoning across tool calls, bound the whole feedback
+  path, keep the per-step preamble small, and test per model and harness: with the model fixed, cost per
+  task differed up to 3x across production harnesses, and for lightweight models success differed 28 to
+  44 points across harnesses.'
+how: '1. Start from the cheapest design that could work and make the loop beat it. If every lookup can
+  be run in advance, a pipeline that gathers them and asks for one decision may match it (90.5% against
+  88.8% correct on banking cases); if the steps are prescribed, a tight fixed-step loop may match a generic
+  one (same success on stepwise GUI tests at 2.6 to 10.7 times lower cost). Use the generic loop where
+  the agent must pick its next step from what it finds or recover from wrong steps; where it reads untrusted
+  content and a trusted API covers the actions, also test compiling the plan before any content is read
+  (6.c.i, 6.c.ii). 2. Use the provider''s native tool calling, not a text Thought/Action/Observation template.
+  3. Loop: call the model, run every tool call it returns, append each result to the history (errors included,
+  as observations), and call again; stop on a final answer or an explicit finish tool. Keep the accumulated
+  history rather than rebuilding a compact prompt each step: on goal-only Google Maps tasks, runs with
+  a wrong tool call still succeeded 78% to 83% of the time in generic loops that kept it, against 24%
+  to 53% in a fixed-step loop that rebuilt its prompt from a step log. 4. Let the model reason before
+  each action (interleaved or adaptive thinking; a think tool on models without it), and pass its reasoning
+  back unchanged across tool calls. 5. Cap steps, cost and wall time on the whole feedback path, retries
+  and handoffs included; count a cap hit as a failure (7.b.iii sizes the caps, 7.b.i catches stalls).
+  6. Keep the per-step preamble (system prompt and tool schemas) small: it and the growing history are
+  re-sent every step, and with the model fixed, cost per task differed up to 3x across production harnesses,
+  set by the preamble (16k tokens to under a thousand) times the step count. 7. Paired eval against the
+  step 1 baseline, per model and harness and separately for prescribed and goal-only tasks, several runs
+  per arm: success scored from the executed actions, not the final message (in one banking study, across five harnesses, 26.1%
+  of violations came with a message denying the executed action), steps, cost and tokens per task, latency,
+  cap-hit rate, and success after a wrong tool call.'
