+  the cases where it was needed, wrong-tool calls, tokens of tool definitions per request, searches per
+  task, latency, billed cost with prompt caching on, and responses cut off at the output cap before any
+  call. 2. Remove tools with no calls and no needing case, and tools the agent''s credentials cannot use.
+  Do not leave a tool in tasks it does not serve: a related but unnecessary tool cut six models'' pooled
+  answer rate on questions they could answer unaided from 98.2% to 63.5% (two models were unaffected);
+  where it must stay, a one-sentence note on its scope raised the answer rate by up to 45.6 points in
+  that study (2.b.ii). 3. Consolidate: find overlapping pairs (similar names or descriptions, or cases
+  routed to the wrong one of the pair) and merge them; rewording cannot separate tools whose scopes genuinely
+  overlap (2.a.ii). Group by task class, with an action or method parameter for closely related operations,
+  but do not collapse a server into one catch-all tool: across nine small local models, four task-level
+  tools completed 0.49 of tasks against 0.42 for 8-10 single-purpose tools, 0.42 for one read and one
+  write tool, and 0.36 for a single tool, with which 28.2% of runs called no tool. 4. If what remains
+  is still large, keep a small core of frequently used tools loaded and defer the rest behind tool search,
+  per-server files or expandable groups of under about 10 tools each. Thresholds differ: Anthropic recommends
+  deferral from 10 tools or 10k tokens of definitions; one vendor''s cost model puts the break-even against
+  a static list at about 25-28 tools; in one benchmark GPT-5.4 lost 0.5 points with 1,000 unrelated tools
+  listed (87.2% to 86.7%) and 3.9 at 1,500, while tool search held level. First check that the model can
+  drive discovery: with GPT-5.1, gateways that exposed tools through discovery scored 14.9 to 27.8 points
+  below flat lists on the same tasks, while with GPT-5.4 they matched (85.1% against 87.2%). 5. Check
+  search on your own requests: build probes from real multi-step tasks, label the full set of tools each
+  needs (including the tools that produce the IDs later calls consume), and score completeness (every
+  needed tool returned), not recall. At 2,048 registered tools a flat lexical retriever returned the needed
+  tools in 19.4% of its shortlists against 77.8% for a router that picked the server first; one vendor''s
+  ranker covered every needed tool on the first search in 304 of 327 sessions, most misses coming from
+  how the agent split the task. Count searches per task: against a 100-tool list one search cut session
+  input a median 28.2%, while three or more raised it 32.3% to 43.8%. 6. Keep the prompt cache intact:
+  keep the loaded core and the order of definitions fixed, and add discovered tools by appending (deferred
+  loading, a tool-addition block) rather than rebuilding the list mid-session (3.b.ii, 3.b.i). Judge cost
+  in dollars with caching on: with 85% to 87% of input served from cache, 17.2% fewer input tokens became
+  a 5.5% lower bill (an interval that includes zero). 7. Measure with a paired eval per model, old tool
+  set against new (and flat list against search where deferring), several runs per arm: success, search
+  completeness, wrong-tool calls, calls and searches per task, tokens, billed cost, latency, and responses
+  truncated at the output cap. Give reasoning models enough output budget for the tool list before blaming
+  the tools: with a 1,024-token cap, going from 8 to 128 tools cost six truncation-heavy configurations
+  43.7 points, against 2.7 at 16,384. Where MCP servers come from third parties, add tool-injection probes:
+  in one benchmark, gateways that exposed tools through discovery held tool-injection attack success at
+  about 16% against 40.6% to 51.5% for flat lists with GPT-5.1, and about 31% against 45.1% to 53.9%
+  with GPT-5.4; with GPT-5.1 discovery also cost up to 5.5 points of task success.'
