+summary: 'Rewrite each tool''s name, parameters and description for the model, starting from the calls
+  it gets wrong. Give each tool a distinct name, a strict schema whose parameter names say what the request
+  supplies, and a description with fixed sections (what it is for and when to use it versus sibling tools,
+  defaults and limits, error modes and what to do next, side effects), plus an example for complex parameters:
+  in one study parameter names and structure swayed five models'' choice between equivalent tools far
+  more than tool names did, examples raised complex-parameter accuracy from 72% to 90% in Anthropic''s
+  testing, and one LLM rewrite fed the misrouted cases matched hand-tuning. Task-level gains are often
+  small: trace-driven description edits cut tool calls 24-33% and cost 18-38% on three production servers
+  but raised the pass rate on only one, description-only rewrites did not significantly lift a customer-service
+  benchmark (one cut a domain from 29.5% to 25.6%), and with informative names, stripping descriptions
+  moved success at most 4 points on nine open models, so slim the result and keep only what a paired test
+  per model confirms. Where a failure needs a lookup or a result the tool does not provide, change the
+  tool itself; where two tools genuinely overlap, rewording cannot separate them: consolidate instead
+  (2.a.i).'
+how: '1. Start from the baseline: from traces or the eval set, collect per tool the misrouted cases (called
+  when it should not have been, not called when it should), malformed or wrong arguments, tool errors
+  and retries, and calls, tokens and cost per task. Label which failures a description could fix: in one
+  study most failures on a customer-service domain were reasoning errors made after the tools had returned
+  what was needed, and failures that needed a lookup or a result the tools did not provide were fixed
+  by changing the tool, which a description cannot do; tool pairs whose scopes genuinely overlap go to
+  2.a.i. 2. Audit names, schemas and descriptions: missing purpose (56% of audited MCP tools) or when-to-use
+  against sibling tools, repeated names (73% in an audit of 10,831 MCP servers), parameters without a
+  type, enum, required flag or description, parameters grouped into one object, and parameter names that
+  signal a different input from what requests supply (renaming a send-email tool''s ''to'' to ''recipient_id''
+  cut its selection to 1-10% across five models, while ''receiver'' kept it at 35-51%). 3. Fix the schema:
+  separate top-level parameters named for what the request supplies (models chose them over one grouped
+  object in 93-100% of trials), a description for each parameter, types, enums and required fields, and
+  the provider''s strict or constrained tool calling where offered (in one study forcing a schema-valid
