+summary: 'Sort every action the agent can take by its side effect (read, reversible write, irreversible
+  or visible to others) and let it run, ask first, or block it by class. Where the environment stages
+  changes (a branch with PR review and CI, a draft), gate only what escapes the stage, and count what
+  takes effect at run time (uploads, deletions, credential reads, install hooks) as escaping. Gates work
+  where the host enforces them: across six coding harnesses with injected instructions, auto-approve let
+  95.6% of attack checks pass against 29.2% with an LLM reviewer (at 18.3 points of task utility), and
+  a command deny list cut attack success from 41.7% to 1.3% with no utility loss, yet an estimated 90%
+  of destructive MCP tools have no server-side confirmation, so build a stage or enforce an ask gate in
+  the host for every tool not marked read-only. Give approvers the action''s effects, not only its text
+  (approvers reading only the command left 14.8% to 18.8% of coding tasks harmed against 19.7% with no
+  approval; one shown a sandboxed pre-run''s effects left 6.7%), keep a deny floor that auto-approve cannot
+  lift, and write the policy as code with tested examples.'
+how: '1. Start from the baseline: list every action the agent can take, by tool and by what each tool
+  runs underneath (package scripts, build targets, interpreters, MCP tools), and mark its side effect:
+  read, reversible write, or irreversible or externally visible (send, pay, delete, publish, push, network
+  calls, credential reads). Treat a tool you have not checked as irreversible: an estimated 90% of destructive
+  tools in a sample of the MCP registry have no server-side confirmation, and the MCP specification tells
+  clients to treat tool annotations as untrusted unless the server is trusted. Run your eval set with
+  auto-approve and count the irreversible actions the agent takes, including ones requested by injected
+  content (with no approval step, four of five low-cost models carried out an injected deletion in 479
+  of 960 runs). 2. For each irreversible action, find where review can happen before it takes effect:
+  an existing stage (a branch with PR review and CI, a draft, a staging environment, pending changes)
+  or none. A stage holds only what waits in it: workspace edits behind PR review are staged, but a command''s
+  run-time effects are not (an upload, an install-hook callback, a deleted untracked file), nor is a write
+  to a file a running system reads. Showing the agent its changes after each command is too late for these:
+  a staging file system that the agent reviewed and reverted itself left 21.2% of 682 shell tasks harmed
+  against 19.7% with no approval, because ''a credential once sent cannot be recalled''. Contain what
+  the agent can reach first (10.a.i), so fewer actions need a gate. 3. Where there is no stage, build
+  one (draft and approve, pending changes, batched approval) or enforce an ask gate in the host, not in
+  the tool''s arguments: given a confirmation flag, models set confirm=true themselves in 127 of 169 injected-deletion
+  attempts. Ask by default for every tool not marked read-only or non-destructive by a source you trust:
+  in one estimate this would leave 5.4% of destructive-tool and application pairs unapproved against 60.7%
+  under current defaults, at the cost of asking on 82.6% of pairs whose flagged tool is harmless, against
+  32.5%. Where no one can answer (headless or scheduled runs), refuse or queue the action. 4. Keep a deny
+  floor that auto-approve cannot lift for what must never run unattended: one harness denies rm -rf, sudo,
+  git push, git reset --hard, chmod -R and chown -R by default; another keeps a twelve-pattern hardline
+  floor that survives --yolo, with the switch frozen at startup so injected content cannot flip it, and
+  matches 47 dangerous patterns on deobfuscated commands. Parse commands and scope ''always allow'' grants
+  to the command and its subcommand (git commit *), not the whole tool. In one benchmark a command deny
+  list cut attack success from 41.7% to 1.3% of checks while utility rose from 94.6% to 99.8%, and an
+  allowlist cut it from 98.1% to 50.0% at 1.5 points of utility, but one agent ran a checker the gate
+  had refused through an allowed Python interpreter: test every path to each protected operation (interpreter,
+  script, file edit, MCP tool). 5. Send the ask class to a human, a rule or an automated approver that
+  fails closed on timeout or malformed output and returns its reason to the agent so it can take a safer
+  path. Give the approver the action''s effects, not only its text: on the same 682 tasks a rule checker,
+  three guardrail models and Claude Code Auto Mode left 14.8% to 18.8% of tasks harmed against 19.7% with
+  no approval, and 62.7% to 94.1% of tasks whose harm hid inside an invoked tool (71.6% with no approval);
+  pre-running each command in a snapshotted sandbox with the network off and giving an LLM approver a
