+summary: 'Run several independent attempts and pick the answer by vote before paying for a verifier. A
+  vote over 16 runs lifted search agents from 59.7% to 75.5%, 8 parallel runs lifted a data agent from
+  73.8% to 81.4% at up to 2.1x cost, and for code, keeping the candidate that passed the most available
+  tests beat a same-model judge (453 against 376 of 500) at lower cost; on math a generative verifier
+  needed 4-64x the compute to match the vote and paid only on hard, medium-difficulty problems from weak-to-medium
+  generators. Verification pays where a vote cannot: on long-horizon tasks that deliver open-ended artifacts
+  a ten-run vote added under a point while a verifier that checked claims against the workspace added
+  about 4, and an ensemble of diverse weak verifiers weighted by estimated accuracy beat both the vote
+  and the best single reward model (87.7% against 72.2% and 72.7%). Any selector is capped by what the
+  attempts cover (a math vote stalled near 43% while coverage reached 98%), so measure coverage first.'
+how: '1. Start from the baseline: run the current agent once per task on a labeled sample and record accuracy,
+  cost and latency, then rerun each task 8 or more times to measure run-to-run disagreement and coverage
+  (the share of tasks at least one run solves). If runs agree and coverage barely exceeds single-run accuracy,
+  stop here: no selector can pick an answer the runs never produce. Run whole attempts in parallel only
+  where each gets a fresh or resettable environment; where actions change shared state, drafting parallel
+  plans and executing one lowered workflow accuracy by 4.2 to 4.6 points in one study. 2. Add N parallel
+  whole-run attempts and submit the most common final answer, grouping equivalent answers (normalized
+  text, equal values) before counting; compare against the single run in a paired eval at matched cost,
+  charging every call in dollars. Expect the gain to taper: on search agents the vote reached 65.5%, 69.7%,
+  73.8% and 75.5% at 2, 4, 8 and 16 runs against 59.7% for one, while coverage reached 90.9%. At a fixed
+  thinking budget, parallel traces with a majority vote beat forcing one trace to think longer. 3. Match
+  the selection rule to the output. For code, run every candidate on the tests you have and keep the one
+  that passes most, ties to the earliest: 453 of 500 correct against 376 for a same-model judge, at 12-49%
+  lower API cost, and consulting the judge only on ties added cost and no accuracy (452). For short answers,
+  vote: on identical math pools a same-model judge beat a three-way vote 186 to 182 of 292 (interval spanning
+  zero) while the vote cost 12-21% less. Whatever the rule, fall back to the first nonempty candidate
+  when selection returns nothing usable; this recovered 61 of 500 coding submissions at unchanged cost
+  and turned an 8.6-point loss against one attempt into a 3.6-point gain. 4. Only then try a verifier
+  or judge, at the same compute and on the hard slice, and keep it only if it beats the vote there. On
+  math a generative verifier needed 4-64x the compute to match the vote. On search agents an LLM aggregator
+  agent scored below the vote (68.9% against 75.5% at 16 runs) and other generative aggregators roughly
+  matched a confidence-weighted vote, while a small selector trained on labeled pools added 3.1 points
+  and, on WebQA, matched a 64-run vote with 8 runs; on GAIA a list-wise selector that compares all candidates
+  beat the vote (63.03 against 56.8 with 4 runs, selection compute not matched). Do not filter a single
+  attempt through a verifier (32.5% to 25.1% resolved on SWE-bench Verified); with several attempts take
+  the top-scoring one (4.4 points above the best fixed threshold at 16 rollouts). Re-asking the same judges
+  helps only when wrong answers do not fool most of them (nine calls instead of one: -0.3 to +0.1 points
+  on MATH-500, +1.2 to +4.8 on GPQA). If you ensemble verifiers, weight diverse weak verifiers by estimated
+  accuracy instead of averaging them (87.7% against 69.1% for a naive top-10 ensemble). 5. For long-horizon
+  agents that deliver open-ended artifacts (reports, spreadsheets, patches), where a vote over whole artifacts
+  barely helps (+0.3 and +0.8 points with ten runs on five workspace benchmarks), spend on verification
+  against the environment: a verifier using the same model that tested disputed and agreed claims against
+  the source files and data added 4.1 to 4.4 points at $1.44 to $3.92 per task. Its gains came where runs
+  disagreed (6.1 points over the pool mean against 0.6 on unanimous pools), and 34% of values all ten
+  runs agreed on were still wrong, so have it challenge the consensus too. 6. Size N and verification
+  from a small labeled pilot rather than a fixed rule, and stop early: a pilot-calibrated plan came within
+  0.02-0.8 points of the best allocation in hindsight where every fixed rule lost at least 2.5 points
+  somewhere, and stopping a task at its first accepted candidate kept success within 0.24 points while
+  spending 5-25% less. A second model adds less than independence suggests (+1.6 points against a predicted
+  +5) because models fail on the same problems, and fixed tests accept more wrong code from stronger generators,
+  so rerun the pilot whenever the model changes. 7. Measure with a paired eval on the same labeled tasks
+  at matched dollar cost: one run, N runs with a vote (or tests), and N runs with the verifier; report
+  coverage, selection accuracy when a correct candidate exists, empty or failed selections, cost and latency,
+  split by difficulty and by whether the runs disagree, and include a stronger model answering once (in
+  one study it beat five cheaper models with extra samples outside programming).'
