-summary: Manage the history before it overflows. Past a soft threshold (about 60% of the window) replace
-  old tool outputs with short stubs; only past a hard threshold (about 85%) summarize the oldest turns
-  into a summary with fixed sections (goal, files touched, done, pending, errors, next step) that is updated
-  incrementally, not regenerated. Keep the recent tail verbatim. The value is large at small windows and
-  near zero at 128k; compaction alone does not carry multi-window work (see 4.b.iii for state kept in files).
-how: 1. Measure peak context against the window on the longest runs. 2. Implement the two thresholds and
-  the fixed-section summary. 3. Evaluate with post-compaction probes (recall, artifacts, next step) and
-  tokens per task, not compression ratio.
+summary: 'Clear old tool outputs, then summarize, only when the context window binds, and carry the task''s
+  rules and the last completed call forward verbatim, because summaries drop them. It pays most at small
+  windows (+35.7 points at 32k against +2.7 at 128k on coding tasks; +12.3 points on long sessions of
+  a reasoning model in a 49K window), while two 1M-window frontier models scored the same with the trigger
+  anywhere from 160K to 380K tokens and no strategy beat their default self-summarization. Do it in stages
+  and in batches: past about 60% of the usable window replace old tool outputs with stubs, past about
+  85% summarize the oldest turns into fixed sections updated in place, keep the recent tail verbatim,
+  and avoid edits every turn (each one forces a re-prefill; trimming every turn cost 1.85 times the full
+  context). The losses are ones benchmarks rarely score: the first summary dropped about one of a task''s
+  three rules, rules kept in the summary were still broken 2.6 times as often as with the full history,
+  and harnesses that flatten roles into text let forged user turns into the summary in 87% to 100% of
+  trials; work that outlives one window needs its state in files (4.b.iii).'
+how: '1. Start from the baseline, per model: on the longest runs, measure peak context against the usable
+  window (the window minus the reply and reasoning reserve), overflow and turn-cap endings, runs that
+  end when one reasoning turn fills the room the history left, success, and tokens and cost per task.
+  Compaction pays where the window binds: managed history added 35.7 points at 32k but 2.7 at 128k on
+  SWE-bench Verified, and for two 1M-window frontier models moving the trigger between 160K and 380K tokens
+  (16% to 38% of the window) did not change scores and no strategy beat the scaffold''s default self-summarization.
+  If runs rarely approach the window, keep the default compaction and spend the effort on what it must
+  not lose (steps 5 and 6) instead. 2. Set two thresholds on the usable window and leave room for a full
+  turn. Past a soft threshold (about 60%) replace old tool outputs with a stub that keeps the tool name,
+  the arguments and a handle to get the content back (a file path, a command to re-run, an ID); re-reading
+  or re-running is the recovery path, since a dedicated recall tool went unused in more than half of the
+  settings tested and did not raise success. Past a hard threshold (about 85%) summarize the oldest turns
+  and keep a verbatim recent tail (about 30% of the window, at least two turns). For reasoning models
+  in tight windows trigger earlier: in a 49K window most uncompacted failures (102 of 200 sessions) were
+  one reasoning turn exhausting the room left, compacting at about half the input budget raised the long-session
+  resolve rate from 41.2% to 52.9%, and a trigger at 48K of a 51.7K budget fired in 4 of 50 sessions and
+  behaved like no compaction. Do not compact earlier than needed to save tokens: a 400K-window model scored
+  0.38 with a 160K trigger against about 0.60 at 260K and above, and summarizing at 48K instead of the
+  standard 96K or 192K threshold lowered the average score of two of three open models (51.0 to 48.3 and
+  44.1 to 37.1). 3. Elide in batches at the threshold, not every turn. Every edit to earlier history rewrites
+  the cached prefix and forces a re-prefill of everything after it: trimming tool outputs to the latest
+  12 rounds on every turn cost 1.85 times as much as keeping the full context. Fire elision only when
+  it frees a large block (OpenCode prunes only when more than 20K tokens are reclaimable and protects
+  the newest 40K; with that rule elision stayed close to self-summarization on PaperBench for every model
+  tested), keep the rest of the prefix byte-stable (3.b.i), and expect each summary to cost one turn of
+  cache reuse (96% against 98% of prompt tokens reusable). On a self-hosted engine, dropping old tool-output
+  KV cache instead of editing the text avoids the re-prefill (27.7% lower pooled cost and +1.7 points
+  against threshold summarization); hosted APIs do not offer it. 4. Summarize into fixed sections and
+  update them in place. Keep sections for the goal, files and other artifacts touched, done, pending,
+  errors and decisions, and the next step; give the summarizer the previous summary plus the newly folded
+  turns and merge, instead of regenerating from the whole history. Task records revised in place scored
+  55.93 (OSWorld partial score) against 50.93 for a summary following the same principles, 44.94 for a
+  generic summary and 50.32 for no compaction on 40 tasks, and anchored iterative summaries beat full
+  regeneration 3.70 to 3.44 on one vendor''s 0-5 probe scale. Write summaries with the agent''s own model
+  or a tested equal: Claude Haiku 4.5 summaries kept Claude Opus 4.6 within its baseline interval but
+  cost GPT-5.4 16 points and GPT-5.3-Codex 20, and removing old reasoning blocks before summarizing cost
+  GPT-5.4 16. Check paths, identifiers, error lines and numbers in the summary against the source and
+  drop any that occur nowhere. Where sessions are time-bound, write the summary in the background and
+  apply it on the next turn: a 27B reasoning writer took a median 161 s per summary, and under a 45-minute
+  session cap the success gain vanished. 5. Carry forward verbatim what a summary cannot be trusted with:
+  the task statement, every operating rule (prohibitions, scope exclusions, approval requirements, process
+  instructions) and the most recent completed call with its result. Summaries drop rules even when the
+  prompt asks to keep them: the first summary dropped on average 0.92 of a task''s three anti-cheating
+  rules and 1.93 by the tenth compaction, because later summaries are written from earlier ones that already
+  lost them, and on real coding sessions process instructions and scope exclusions were dropped 35.9%
+  and 36.8% of the time against 6.2% for feature requirements. Re-inject the original task and rule messages
+  after each compaction (one published harness re-injects prior user messages for this) or keep them in
+  a file the agent re-reads (4.b.iii), and tell the summarizer to preserve rules with their scope, exceptions
+  and negation: in one benchmark that instruction was the only compaction strategy below the full-history
+  violation rate (9.0% against 11.9%). Never compact by dropping whole turns (first-in-first-out lost
+  the rule in every run) or into a summary budget of a few hundred tokens (at 300 and 200 tokens at least
+  95.1% of runs lost it). Keeping the rule is not enough: runs whose summary kept it still broke it 2.6
+  times as often as full-history runs, so enforce rules that matter outside the model (10.b.i). Removing
+  the last completed call raised immediate repeats of it from 3.3% to 48.8%, and an explicit trace or
+  ledger of past actions in the summary did not stop loops (52.0% to 62.7% of runs against 57.3%), so
+  keep the call itself in the tail (7.b.i). 6. Treat the summarizer''s input as a trust boundary and keep
+  the raw history. Pass the history role-typed, or escape role labels inside tool output, rather than
+  flattening it into text such as ''[User]:'': forged user turns planted in tool output became user instructions
+  in the summary in 87% to 100% of trials in five harnesses that flatten roles, against 0% in four that
+  keep messages typed, and a laundered approval led an agent to run an attacker''s command in 3 of 6 live
+  sessions (0 of 5 clean). A prompt rule against injected instructions stopped only 13% to 25% of laundering;
+  a code check of the summary''s user-attributed claims against the stored user turns caught 77% with
+  2% of legitimate claims demoted. Keep the full history in an append-only log or a linked session for
+  audit and recovery and tell the agent where it is, but don''t expect a score gain from it: writing the
+  pre-compaction context to a file did not beat plain self-summarization and lowered one model from 0.38
+  to about 0.24. 7. Measure on the same long tasks and model, several runs per arm: the default or no
+  compaction, elision only, and staged; report success, overflow and cap endings, tokens and cost per
+  task including summarizer calls, cache-hit rate around compactions, time per compaction and wall-clock.
+  Add probes a benchmark score misses: a rule stated once early and a later request that would break it
+  (graded from the tool calls against a full-history run, not from the summary), recall of exact identifiers
+  and files changed, completed work described as pending, repeats of the last completed call, and a forged
+  user line planted in a tool output. Re-run per model: summarizer and strategy effects that held for
+  one model did not transfer to another.'
