+# 9.c.iv: Write a fail-to-pass test before the fix, and harden it against wrong fixes
+# Register entry. Schema: ../schema/entry.schema.json. Vocabulary: ../vocabulary.yaml.
+id: 9.c.iv
+slug: test-first-fail-to-pass
+name: Write a fail-to-pass test before the fix, and harden it against wrong fixes
+kind: technique
+status: card
+tiers:
+- tier: 3
+  why: it changes what the agent does and what it spends, so quality and cost must be checked
+  proposed: true
+levers:
+- control-flow
+domains:
+- code-generation
+evidence_domains:
+- code-generation
+categories:
+- verification.spend
+summary: 'Make a coding agent''s test a real check: write it before any source edit, admit it only if it fails
+  on the unpatched code, then harden it against plausible wrong fixes or have a separate strong model write
+  it and hold it fixed while the agent repairs. A test written together with the fix shares the fix''s mistakes
+  and adds little (58.9% to 61.4% resolved for GPT-5-mini); written first and hardened, it reached 69.4%, at
+  $0.84 against $0.39 per issue. Frozen tests from GPT-5.6-sol raised it from 81.5% to 85.7% on SWE-bench Verified
+  but only 0.7 points on SWE-bench Pro, and tests from a weak test writer lowered a repair agent from 61.2%
+  to 57.3%.'
+how: '1. Start from the current agent as the baseline: resolve rate, cost and steps per issue with no test,
+  and, for each check that passed after the agent''s first edit, whether it also passes when replayed on the
+  unpatched code (46.0% did for GPT-5.6-sol in one study). 2. Have the agent write the test before any source
+  edit, run it on the unpatched code and reject it unless it fails there. Asserting the exact expected behavior
+  matters more than printing values. In one study with GPT-5-mini, a test written together with the fix gave
+  61.4% resolved against 58.9% without, at $0.56 against $0.39 per issue; joint test and fix failures ran at
+  1.87 times the rate expected if they were independent, and 48.2% of unresolved runs had their own test passing
+  on a wrong fix. Dropping the test-first phase cost 7.0 points. 3. Harden the test against plausible wrong
+  fixes: run it against a few mutated versions of the fix (symptom suppressed, incomplete fix, input-specific
+  shortcut) and strengthen it until they fail. Injected reproduction tests that also passed wrong fixes added
+  nothing to the fix rate (+0.0 points) while strict ones added 8.5; the full loop took GPT-5-mini from 58.9%
+  to 69.4%, at $0.84 against $0.39 per issue. 4. Alternatively, separate and freeze: a separate run writes
+  the test from the issue, the harness admits it only if it fails on the unpatched code, and the repair agent
+  then edits source only, with the test held fixed. First check the test writer on past issues: its tests should
+  fail on the unpatched code and pass on the known fix (87.8% of SWE-bench Verified issues for GPT-5.6-sol,
+  61.0% for Codex-5.3, 22.2% for an untrained Qwen3.5-35B-A3B). Frozen tests from GPT-5.6-sol raised a Qwen
+  repair agent from 61.2% to 65.3%, while tests from the untrained Qwen model lowered it to 57.3%. 5. A cheaper
+  step on its own: have the harness tell the agent when a passing check also passes on the unpatched code (a
+  median 11.0 s per run). It cut runs closing on such checks from 23.8% to 13.6% but did not change the resolve
+  rate. 6. Measure with paired runs against the no-test baseline at matched spend where you can, several runs
+  per arm: resolve rate, cost and steps per issue, and the share of kept tests that fail on the unpatched code
+  and reject mutated fixes.'
+detect:
+  static: a test step that runs after or alongside the fix, with no check that the test fails on the unpatched
+    code
+  traces: the agent's own test passing on a fix that the reference tests reject; tests that also pass on the
+    unpatched code; runs that submit right after their own new test passes
+  evals: resolve rate with and without the test step at matched spend; for a separate test writer, the share
