+summary: 'Write down when the agent should use each tool, and put it where the model always reads it.
+  For each under-used or misused tool or skill, add a rule to the system prompt or AGENTS.md naming the
+  situation, the tool and what it replaces (e.g. the dedicated read or edit tool rather than bash), instead
+  of leaving it inside a skill the agent must choose to open: an explicit AGENTS.md instruction took a
+  docs skill that was never invoked in 56% of cases from a 53% to a 79% pass rate, and in another study
+  a rule loaded only on demand kept about half of its effect. Test the wording against no rule and an
+  irrelevant rule of the same length, since results swing with it: a one-line skill reminder did nothing
+  where a per-skill YES/NO check reached 100% activation, telling models to call tools ''only if necessary''
+  changed almost nothing while ''use sparingly'' also cut needed calls, and a scope instruction that recovered
+  most of the answers an unnecessary tool had cost reduced one model''s needed tool use. It will not make
+  a model that rarely calls tools start calling them, and in one coding study random rule files helped
+  as much as curated ones, so measure the rule''s own effect on call rates, not only pass rate.'
+how: '1. Start from the baseline: on the eval set (or recorded sessions), log per tool and skill the call
+  rate on cases where its trigger condition holds and where it does not, wrong-tool calls (bash used to
+  read, search or edit files where a dedicated tool exists; the wrong one of two overlapping tools), pass
+  rate, tokens, turns and latency. Include cases that need the tool, phrased with and without its obvious
+  keywords, and cases that do not: in one practitioner test a skill fired about 100% of the time when
+  the prompt held its keyword, 20-40% on generic phrasing and about 0% on indirect phrasing, and in one
+  benchmark open models made more than one tool call per task by default, even on tasks they could answer
+  directly. 2. Pick the targets from those traces: tools or skills rarely called where they would have
+  helped (a docs skill was never invoked in 56% of one vendor''s eval cases), tools called where they
+  are not needed or where a dedicated one exists, and questions a tool''s mere presence made the model
+  refuse or get wrong (one related but unnecessary tool cut answer rates from 98.2% to 63.5% across six
+  models). If the model rarely calls any tool, a rule will not fix it (weak models barely searched when
+  told to), so change the model (1.b.i) rather than the wording. 3. Write one rule per target: the situation,
+  the tool, and what it replaces (e.g. to read or search files, use the Read and Grep tools instead of
+  cat or grep in Bash; for questions about X use tool A, for Y tool B), naming the exact tool, command
+  or path. In one study of rules added to real Skills, the gain came mainly from rules that named a command
+  or path the agent had not been given, and stating the rule added 0.15 compliance beyond merely mentioning
+  the token. Where a tool is over-used, name the cases that do not need it rather than adding a blanket
+  instruction: across six open models ''only if necessary'' changed almost nothing (-1.0 points, 0.06
+  fewer calls per task) and ''expensive, use sparingly'' cut needed calls too, costing 8.4 points of accuracy
+  (11.1 on hard tasks). 4. Put the rules where the model always reads them (system prompt, AGENTS.md or
+  CLAUDE.md), not in an optional skill body: with the rule inside a Skill the agent had to choose to open,
+  four coding agents kept 51% of its effect on the required action on average (three open models 38%,
+  Claude Sonnet 4.5 all of it). Generate the rules from the active toolset, as Pi does with per-tool prompt
+  snippets, so the prompt never names a tool that is not offered, and keep them short: carrying a whole
+  Skill body in context cost 37-57% more tokens per episode. If triggers still fail, re-inject a short
+  check each turn: a hook that made Claude Code state YES or NO for each skill before acting took activation
+  from 50-55% to 100% on 22 prompts in two runs, where a one-line reminder (50-59%) and the harness''s
+  built-in prompt hook (41-55%) did not help. 5. Check both directions before keeping a rule: a scope
+  instruction that restored 27.8 to 45.6 points of answers on four models cut GPT-OSS 20B''s tool use
+  where the tool was needed from 94.4% to 72.4%, and asking the model to reason about need before every
+  call collapsed tool calling on two Llama models (Llama-3.3-70B accuracy 83.1% to 47.9%). 6. A/B two
+  or more wordings against no rule and against an irrelevant rule of the same length, with several runs
+  per arm, because a gain may not come from the rule''s content: in one coding study random rule files
+  matched curated ones (63.8% each against a 50.0% baseline), while another found same-length unrelated
+  text had no effect. Keep the wording with the highest trigger recall and pass rate and no rise in false
+  triggers or in failures on previously passing cases, and drop rules the model already follows or ignores
+  (one rule-model pair in five added tokens with no detected gain). 7. Measure: run the paired eval per
+  model and re-run it when the model, harness or toolset changes; report per tool the trigger recall and
+  false-trigger rate, the wrong-tool rate, pass rate overall and on cases that need the tool, and tokens,
+  turns and latency; track the same call rates in production traces.'
