+summary: 'Give the agent one tool that holds a task list (a status per item, one item in progress), require
+  the list on the first turn of non-trivial tasks, and show the current list every turn instead of keeping
+  old copies in history. It pays mainly for models that would otherwise stop early or drop work: in controlled
+  studies it added 11.6 points on SWE tasks for a 30B model and kept an open 120B model working through
+  long browser workflows (0.18 lower, to about zero, without it), while strong models gained little (0.77
+  to 1.36 points on repository generation) or traded about 2 points of success for about 30% lower cost
+  on SWE tasks. Writing a list is not enough on its own: a TODO list the model wrote once at the start
+  moved workspace pass rate between -3.90 and +1.66 points across three models, while a record kept by
+  the harness, with done marks checked against what the agent read and a finish check of open items, gained
+  3.10 to 15.49 points (in the one model ablated, the gain disappeared without the harness''s own capture of reads and its checks). Updates
+  cost tokens and actions (21.10% more prompt tokens for one model; maybe a third of actions in some tasks,
+  by one vendor''s account), so measure per model and re-run after a model change.'
+how: '1. Start from the baseline: run your eval set per model with no list and record success, cost, tokens,
+  turns, runs that end without the required output (no edit, no answer file) or at a timeout, and items
+  the task asks for that are missing from the output. Expect the list to matter where runs end early or
+  drop items: without a plan, a 30B coding model ended 68.6% of SWE runs without an edit (27.8% with one)
+  at a median of 5 turns (40 with one); an open 120B model''s browser workflows fell from a median of
+  176.5 to 63 turns; and a plain workspace agent left about one in five numeric values it had been shown
+  (19.8%) out of its deliverable. Report the answer rate next to the score: at similar scores, a 35B model
+  without a plan left 47 of 96 tool-task episodes without the required output against 24 with one, and
+  timed out twice as often (44 against 22). 2. Add one task-list tool whose every call re-sends the complete
+  list (each item with its text and a status: pending, in_progress or completed; exactly one in_progress),
+  and have the harness validate each call (item format, allowed statuses, a length cap such as 10 items,
+  consistency) and return a clear error when it fails. Require it on non-trivial tasks: a system instruction
+  to make the first action a list for any task of about three or more steps (skip a single trivial step
+  or a purely informational request), a reminder each turn until a list exists, and an instruction to
+  mark an item completed as soon as it is actually done, not in batches. 3. Keep only the latest list
+  in the harness and show it fresh before every model call, never as a copy in history: one study appended
+  it to the end of the model input as a reminder block, another appended a one-line status of open items
+  to each tool result. No source compares the end of the context with a block after the system prompt
+  or a file; appending at the end leaves the cached prefix unchanged (3.b.i). If the list has not been
+  updated for several turns, inject a reminder to update it. 4. Keep updates cheap and count them: the
+  list costs actions and tokens. In one coding study it raised prompt tokens 21.10% for one model and
+  3.51% for another; Manus''s co-founder recalled that maybe a third of actions in some of its tasks went
+  to updating a todo file, and Manus moved planning to a separate planner agent. Log list updates as a
+  share of all actions; if the share is large, ask for updates only when an item changes status, or move
+  planning to a planner. 5. Keep the list honest: a done mark should rest on evidence the harness can
+  check, not on the model''s say-so. Have the agent cite what completes an item (a passing test, a written
+  file, an excerpt it read) and refuse a done that cites nothing relevant; let the harness record what
+  it can observe itself (files read, files listed but never opened) instead of asking the model to write
+  it down; and before the agent stops, return the items still open (9.b.iii). In one workspace study a
+  TODO list the model wrote at the start moved pass rate between -3.90 and +1.66 points across three models,
+  while a harness-kept record of requirements, reads and unopened files, with evidence-checked done marks
+  and a finish check, gained 3.10 to 15.49 points; removing the evidence check or the finish check each
+  cost 1.84 points, and without the harness''s own record of reads (and the review and evidence check
+  built on it) the pass rate fell to 41.36, level with the plain agent''s 41.63. 6. Measure per model
+  and task type, with several runs per arm, with and without the list: success and answer rate, cost,
+  tokens, turns, timeouts, list updates as a share of actions, items marked done without evidence and
+  items still open at the finish. Expect the sign to depend on the model: the list added 11.6 points on
+  SWE tasks for a 30B model that otherwise stopped early, 0.77 to 1.36 points for two strong models on
+  repository generation, and no clear score change for a 35B model on clinical tasks, and it cost strong
+  coding models 2.0 and 0.4 points of success while cutting their SWE cost about 30% by trimming post-edit
+  verification. Re-run it when the model changes.'
