-summary: 'An agent''s own history, and any examples in its prompt, act as few-shot demonstrations: when
-  the context fills with near-identical action-observation pairs (twenty similar items in a row, a retried
-  call), the model tends to copy their surface form instead of judging the current item. The change is
-  to break that uniformity: vary the serialization, phrasing and order of examples and of newly appended
-  actions and observations, describe repeated or failed actions instead of replaying them verbatim, or
-  move independent items into separate contexts (5.c.ii). The measured support is mostly for the mechanism
-  and for diverse examples: a failed call''s verbatim string accounted for 83% of small models'' pull
-  toward repeating it, batching 20 studies flipped 5.6-26.7% of one model''s screening decisions against
-  one-at-a-time runs, and diverse demonstrations beat the most similar ones by up to 23 points. No study
-  tests Manus-style serialization noise on an agent end to end, and equivalent formats alone moved accuracy
-  by up to 76 points, so measure the variation instead of assuming it helps.'
+summary: 'Keep the model from copying its own context: when it fills with near-identical steps or items,
+  vary how new entries are written, label or describe repeats instead of replaying them, and give independent
+  items their own context. An agent''s own history and its prompt examples act as few-shot demonstrations:
+  in one study agents attended more to their own earlier responses as context grew, and periodically clearing
+  old rounds beat keeping the full history on eight agent tasks (68.9% against 54.4% for Qwen3-8B, 71.1%
+  against 64.6% for GPT-4o-mini). Separating items pays most when many similar ones would share a context:
+  grading 25 per prompt changed 11-22% of decisions between random batches, and a majority vote over nine
+  orders of each batch still left 52-60% of those flips; for prompt examples, diverse demonstrations beat
+  the most similar ones by up to 23 points. Changes to how history is written help only a little on current
+  agents and have limits: labeling each observation as the outcome of the action before it raised success
+  from 46.39% to 48.25% and cut repeats from 36.91% to 32.23% across five models, keeping only the last
+  three steps cost the same models 10.4 points, seven current search agents (including Opus 5 and GPT-5.6
+  Sol) still switched strategy 3.6-17.4 points less after a dead-end history than after a neutral one
+  of the same length, and no study tests Manus-style serialization noise end to end, so measure the variation
+  instead of assuming it helps.'
