+  in a second random arrangement and with renamed item keys, since accuracy can hold (AUROC moved -0.033
+  to +0.010) while 11-22% of decisions change with the batch. 2. Find where uniformity enters: tool results
   and actions serialized with one fixed template, harness messages with identical wording, few-shot examples
-  that share one format and one structure, many similar items in one context, and failed calls replayed
-  verbatim. 3. Diversify the examples in the prompt: choose examples that differ in structure and cover
-  edge cases instead of the most similar ones; if you also vary their formatting (field names, separators,
-  layout), pin the required output format with an instruction or a schema, since examples are what the
-  model copies the format from. 4. Vary newly appended history, deterministically: rotate two or three
-  equivalent templates and phrasings for observations and harness messages, and the order of fields that
-  carry no meaning, choosing the variant from the item index or a seed at append time; never rewrite earlier
-  entries, so the context stays append-only and the prefix cache-stable (see 3.b.i). 5. Do not replay repeated
-  or failed actions verbatim (see 7.b.ii): replace a failed call with a short runtime-generated description
-  of what failed, or block exact repeats at the decoder (see 7.b.i); an instruction not to repeat did not
-  reduce repeats, and deleting the failed step made them worse. 6. Where items are independent, break
-  the repetition structurally instead: process each item or small batches in fresh contexts (5.c.ii), shuffle
-  item order across batches, and in proposal loops reject proposals too similar to recently accepted ones;
-  raising the temperature did not add real diversity. 7. Measure with paired runs on the same items, uniform
-  against varied (and against per-item contexts): accuracy and outlier detection by position, decision
-  flip rate against the single-item runs, exact-repeat rate, output-format errors, tokens and cache hit
+  that share one format and one structure, many similar items in one context, failed calls replayed verbatim,
+  and long runs of unproductive steps that all follow one strategy. 3. Diversify the examples in the prompt:
+  choose examples that differ in structure and cover edge cases instead of the most similar ones; if you
+  also vary their formatting (field names, separators, layout), pin the required output format with an
+  instruction or a schema, since examples are what the model copies the format from. 4. Vary and label
+  newly appended history, deterministically: rotate two or three equivalent templates and phrasings for
+  observations and harness messages, and the order of fields that carry no meaning, choosing the variant
+  from the item index or a seed at append time; label each observation as the outcome of the action that
+  produced it ("Outcome of <action>: ..."), which raised success from 46.39% to 48.25% and cut third repeats
+  from 36.91% to 32.23% across five current models; never rewrite or reorder earlier entries (shuffling
+  past actions cost 2.55 points), so the context stays append-only and the prefix cache-stable (see 3.b.i).
+  5. Do not replay repeated or failed actions verbatim (see 7.b.ii): replace a failed call with a short
+  runtime-generated description of what failed, or block exact repeats at the decoder (see 7.b.i); on
+  small models an instruction not to repeat did not reduce repeats and deleting the failed step made them
+  worse, while on five current models a short caution that earlier steps may be wrong, incomplete or stale
+  and that "Repetition is not evidence of correctness" raised success from 46.39% to 48.60%. Do not have
+  the agent write its own lessons into the history as the fix: those notes cut repeats the most (to 21.38%)
+  but lowered success (45.01%). 6. Where items are independent, break the repetition structurally: give
+  each ambiguous item its own context, with all shared instructions first so they are served from the
+  prompt cache (see 5.c.ii); shuffling is not a substitute, since a majority vote over nine orders of
+  a batch left 52-60% of the flips and batches of five still differed from single-item grading on 9-16%
+  of decisions; if you must batch, shuffle items across batches and report agreement with single-item
+  runs. In long sessions on tasks whose state is in the current observation, try clearing old rounds periodically
+  (for example back to the last round once the history reaches 12 rounds) instead of keeping all of it
