+summary: 'Before an agent stops, check its claim that it is done against evidence it cannot write (the
+  environment''s end state, tests it cannot edit, or a separate reviewer that runs the result) and send
+  it back with what failed. Unchecked claims are often wrong: across seven current models on SkillsBench,
+  completion claims exceeded official pass rates by 28.7 to 37.9 points, and letting only trusted validator
+  evidence finalize a run cut that gap to 12.8 points and raised the pass rate from 61.1% to 73.1% at
+  1.53x tokens (one study, repair included); even a cheap check that read only the dialogue withheld 61%
+  of invalid customer-service completions. Who checks matters: gates that reopened the work for the same
+  agent gained -0.5 to +6.2 points at up to 1.76x cost on coding tasks, a separate critic that ran the
+  deliverable gained 3.9 to 9.5 points at 1.00 to 1.25x, and a verifier reading only the agent''s response
+  never passed 0.70 F1 where one checking state and trace reached 0.96. Veto a stop when code changed
+  with no check since, re-verify sub-agent findings, and keep the checker and its checklist out of the
+  agent''s reach, since rules and self-written tests it can see are weak; expect false vetoes (17% and
+  41% of correct runs withheld in one study) and know that a guard that only refuses to stop finished
+  none of the work-unit tasks in another unless the harness also tracked what remained.'
+how: '1. Start from the baseline: score recorded runs or an eval set from the environment (hidden tests,
+  end state, the official evaluator), never from the agent''s message, and log false successes (the agent
+  says done, the check fails), premature stops (required work missing, or the run ends after an edit with
+  no test or build), success, tokens, turns and wall-clock. Expect a gap: across seven current models
+  on SkillsBench, completion claims exceeded official pass rates by 28.7 to 37.9 points, and in Terminal-Bench
+  runs only 6.2% to 13.6% of editing turns were followed by a check. 2. Define done per task type as checks
+  the agent cannot write: tests or a build it cannot edit, the end state (records, files, a deployed endpoint),
+  an end-to-end run through the real interface (browser automation for a web app), and for review tasks
+  the transcript (files claimed as reviewed against files opened). Turn the task''s stated requirements
+  into such checks; let a check block the stop only after you have confirmed it accepts correct work,
+  and keep vague or subjective requirements advisory: in one study, letting unqualified validators block
+  too kept 90.3% of the tasks the unguarded agent had passed, against 96.8%. 3. Enforce it in the harness
+  at the stop: a stop hook or verify-on-stop guard intercepts the final answer, runs the checks, and on
+  failure returns which checks failed and what remains, with a cap on rounds and a logged release at the
+  cap (the critic study allowed one revision). Refuse to stop when files changed after the last passing
+  check, and re-run checks whose inputs changed: without that, all 248 targeted later edits in one study
+  left stale passing evidence accepted. Refusing to stop is not enough on its own: in one study such a
+  guard completed none of the work-unit tasks, while a controller that also tracked pending units completed
+  25% to 50% (7.b.iv). 4. Where the check needs judgment, give it to a separate checker instead of asking
+  the agent to recheck: a critic in a fresh context with read-only access to the live environment, given
+  the task and the diff, that tries to falsify each claim by running the deliverable and returns findings
+  for revision. Across seven coding models this added 3.9 to 9.5 points at 1.00 to 1.25x cost, against
+  -0.5 to +6.2 points at up to 1.76x for gates that reopened the work for the same agent; adding the full
+  trajectory did not help consistently and raised critic cost 29% to 43%. Have an LLM checker fetch evidence
+  for each claim from state and trace with bounded queries, never only the agent''s message (a response-only
+  judge never passed 0.70 F1, a state-and-trace verifier reached 0.96), and before a finding is reported,
+  reproduce it live and have a fresh-context agent replay it (25% fewer false positives, 95% CI 14% to
+  38%, in one vendor''s test). Re-verify sub-agent reports the same way (6.b.iii). 5. Keep the checker
+  and what it reads out of the agent''s write reach: tests and checker configuration read-only or hidden
+  (see 9.a.i on tests agents edit or special-case), the checklist as JSON with only a status field the
+  agent may change, results in a store only the harness writes, and audit scores not shown back (in one
+  study a sealed audit raised results for all six models and revealing its scores lowered them for five).
+  Ask for a scope statement in the final report (what was checked and what was not), but do not rely on
+  it: when asked, only 19.6% of incomplete runs admitted the gap. 6. Set the guard by the cost of each
+  error: a check that read only the dialogue withheld 61% of invalid customer-service completions for
+  under one cent per episode, but also 17% and 41% of correct ones in two domains; where a wrong ''done''
+  is cheap, use a lighter check or send blocked runs to review, and where it is expensive, accept more
+  false vetoes. 7. Measure against the baseline on the same tasks with several runs per arm: false-success
+  and premature-stop rates, success, correct runs blocked, guard firings and rounds per run, runs released
+  at the cap, tokens and wall-clock; re-run after a model change.'
